		{"id":"422746d1f20fd802","type":"text","text":"The best part of my day is ______ because I can ______.","x":7415,"y":7660,"width":378,"height":60,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"d8d9e8eb932433f0","type":"text","text":"It makes me feel ______.","x":7855,"y":7683,"width":250,"height":60,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"d7298074abe800a9","type":"text","text":"LOVED","x":8136,"y":7926,"width":100,"height":60},
		{"id":"50d017b87849a25f","type":"text","text":"LISTENED","x":8186,"y":7600,"width":120,"height":60},
		{"id":"d670ecb94bc02262","type":"text","text":"RELAXED","x":8216,"y":7776,"width":125,"height":60},
		{"id":"8534d03ad72a13cd","type":"text","text":"JOYFUL","x":8224,"y":7685,"width":101,"height":60},
		{"id":"dcc165f5587c5b22","type":"text","text":"COMFORTABLE","x":8245,"y":7866,"width":160,"height":60},
		{"id":"053b90dd5897def8","type":"text","text":"HAPPY","x":8364,"y":7685,"width":91,"height":60},
		{"id":"b9c46a0e90bb6541","type":"text","text":"MR. MIKE:\nTHE BEST PART OF MY DAY IS:\n- VERY SIMPLE : **AROUND 6-7PM** OF **THE 10TH** OF *EVERY MONTH* - BECAUSE I RECEIVE THE SALARY PAYMENT MES-SAGE FROM THE BANK ","x":8760,"y":6320,"width":320,"height":220,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"6b9c722862254085","type":"text","text":"time / moment \n+ activity \n- reason \n\n- feelings","x":7840,"y":7420,"width":160,"height":259,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"039d1c02da72f8a0","type":"text","text":"MRS. LILY \nTHE BEST PART OF MY DAY IS:\n- **IN THE EVENING** \n\t- BECAUSE I HAVE A LOT OF TIME FOR MY FAMILY \n\t\t- I CAN PREPARE DINNER WITH MY CHILDREN \n\t\t-  LISTEN AND SHARE WITH MY KIDS ABOUT OUR DAYS - THIS MAKES ME UNDERSTAND MY KIDS BETTER  ","x":8320,"y":6300,"width":426,"height":320,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"bb90492af198bec8","type":"text","text":"\nWHAT IS **THE BEST PART** OF YOUR DAY?","x":8740,"y":6700,"width":340,"height":60,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"26c3f2b3141dd8bd","type":"text","text":"COMP-TỜ-BỒ","x":9218,"y":6520,"width":150,"height":50},
		{"id":"84bf139929d8bfb0","type":"text","text":"MRS TANYA:\nTHE BEST PART OF MY DAY IS:\n**IN THE EVENING**\n- AFTER DINNER WITH MY FAMILY \n\t- WE WILL PLAY TOGETHER - WATCH TV TOGETHER - AND SHARE ABOUT OUR DAYS AND WHAT'S SPECIAL ABOUT IT\n- WHEN I LISTEN TO MY CHILDREN - I COULD SEE PARTS OF MY YOUNGER SELF MANY YEARS BEFORE IN THEIR EYES - THIS MAKES ME COMFOR-TA-BLE AND HAPPY ","x":9101,"y":6140,"width":385,"height":360},
		{"id":"b39095181e80f49b","type":"text","text":"MRS SOPHIA\nTHE BEST PART OF MY DAY IS:\n- **EARLY IN THE MORNING** - WHEN I WAKE UP AT 5 AM EVERY DAY \n\t- BECAUSE I CAN DO YOGA AT AROUND 5:15 IN THE MORNING \n\t- THEN I HAVE BREAKFAST WITH MY FAMILY AT AROUND 7:00 AM\n- **IN THE EVENING** \n\t- WHEN I CAN \n\t\t- PLAY PICKLEBALL WITH MY FRIENDS \n\t\t- OR HANG-OUT WITH MY FAMILY ","x":7960,"y":6660,"width":420,"height":391,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"860645e62b2fe41f","type":"text","text":"MRS. JENNY\nTHE BEST PART OF MY DAY IS:\n- I FEEL COMFORTABLE WHEN I RELAX WITH A CUP OF COFFEE ***EVERY MORNING*** \n- AND **WHEN I FINISH WORKS ON TIME** \n- ***IN THE EVENING*** WHEN I HAVE MORE TIME TO PLAY WITH MY FAMILY, SUCH AS - SOCCER / OR WATCH TV TOGETHER \n\t- OR **WHEN I HAVE TIME TO HAVE LUNCH** AND DO THE HOUSEWORK TOGETHER \n\t- AND **WHEN I HAVE TIME TO IMPROVE MY ENGLISH SKILLS** \n\t- AND I DO SKIN CARE / APPLY SKINCARE PRODUCTS ","x":8410,"y":6893,"width":500,"height":365,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"bb66cbc6287af382","type":"text","text":"MRS. LUNA\nTHE BEST PART OF MY DAY IS:\n- WHEN I HAVE DINNER WITH MY FAMILY ***AFTER COMING BACK HOME***\n\t- BECAUSE I CAN HAVE TIME TO WATCH THE NEWS ON TV\n\t- THEN I GO TO MY MOM'S HOUSE - NEAR MY HOUSE \n\t- THEN I COME BACK HOME AND RELAX WITH MUSIC \n- **SOME AFTERNOONS**, I WOULD HAVE TIME TO PLAY PICKLEBALL WITH MY FRIENDS","x":8920,"y":6893,"width":380,"height":400,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"0c68e9cc2f490f3f","type":"text","text":"MRS. MARRY\nTHE BEST PART OF MY DAY IS:\n- IS ***THE EARLY MORNING*** BECAUSE I CAN ENJOY MY COFFEE AND LISTEN TO MY FAVORITE SONGS OR WATCH SOME NEWS(PAPER)\n\t- THIS IS HOW I USUALLY START A NEW DAY \n\t- IT MAKES ME FEEL RELAXED AND ENERGETIC FOR WORK AND GIVE BETTER SUPPORT \n- HOWEVER - I ALSO LIKE ***LATE AFTERNOONS***, WHEN I SOMETIMES PLAY PICKLEBALL WITH MY TEAM BECAUSE IT HELPS ME ***STAY FIT AND HEALTHY***\n- AND I ALSO **ENJOY THE EVENING** WHEN I HAVE DINNER WITH MY FAMILY\n\t- AFTER THAT I WATCH TV / MOVIES WITH MY HUSBAND BECAUSE WE HAVE SAME INTERESTS","x":9320,"y":6826,"width":420,"height":500,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"f45591de8491127f","type":"text","text":"MRS SUNNY:\nTHE BEST PART OF MY DAY IS:\n**EARLY IN THE MORNING**\n- WHEN I WAKE UP TO MY SON'S SMILES \n- OR WHEN WE RIDE BICYCLE AND WATCH THE SUNRISE TOGETHER \n- THIS MAKES ME VERY HAPPY AND ENERGETIC AT THE START OF EVERYDAY ","x":9368,"y":6570,"width":400,"height":240},
		{"id":"d947e93826ffa0e5","type":"text","text":"homework 3: read aloud this passage + record it back to the group","x":5840,"y":5360,"width":250,"height":100,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"16b094a1f055ae75","type":"text","text":"rhy**th**m \nfreedom","x":5215,"y":12891,"width":250,"height":104},
		{"id":"6226ef8f7edc6dff","type":"text","text":"pre\\sent \n- noun: mon qua\n- verb: trinh bay - thuyet trinh ","x":5180,"y":13071,"width":290,"height":180},
		{"id":"61863a6ac69cc4be","type":"text","text":"CROSSCHECK","x":5520,"y":13161,"width":250,"height":58},
		{"id":"2219d4a76bacc0c8","type":"text","text":"HOMEWORK:\nCẢI THIỆN CÁC CÂU QUÁ \"DIRECT\" NÀY THÀNH CÁC CÂU REQUEST MANG TÍNH PHỐI HỢP \nDIRECT REQUEST -> COLLABORATIVE REQUEST","x":5840,"y":13251,"width":250,"height":195},
		{"id":"178e5bfc7295dbeb","type":"text","text":"**PPJ BUSINESS ENGLISH SPEAKING & LISTENING**\n\n**SESSION 7: FOLLOWING UP WITHOUT SOUNDING PUSHY**\n\nLevel: A1–A2  \n**Workplace theme: Pending responses and missing confirmations**  \nSpeaking outcome: Bạn có thể **hỏi tiến độ, nhắc lại yêu cầu** và **xin thời gian phản hồi** mà **không tạo cảm giác trách móc hoặc thúc ép**.\n\nTODAY I CAN\n\n□ I can follow up on a pending task politely.  \n□ I can ask for a clear status update.  \n□ I can explain why I need the information.  \n□ I can request a realistic response time.  \n□ I can agree on the next action.\n\n1. READING\n\n**A Professional Follow Up**\n\nThao works in the Import–Export Department. \nOn Monday morning, she asks a supplier to confirm the shipment date for PO 4582. \nThe supplier says they will send the confirmation *before Tuesday afternoon*.\n\nBy Wednesday morning, Thao still has no reply. \nThe Warehouse team needs the delivery information to ***prepare space for the goods.*** \nThe Production team also needs to ***update its work plan.***\n\nThao first wants to write:\n\n“Why haven’t you replied? Send the confirmation now.”\n\nHowever, **this message sounds angry and may create tension**. \nIt focuses on the supplier’s mistake, but it ***does not explain the business problem.***\n\nThao writes a new message:\n\n“Good morning, Mr. Lee. I’m following up on the shipment date con-fir-ma-tion for PO 4582. \nOur Warehouse and Production teams need the information to update their plans. \nCould you please let us ***know the current status*** by eleven thirty today? \nPlease let me know if you need any information from our side. \nThank you for your support.”\n\nThe supplier apologizes for the delay. \nThey are waiting for final approval from the carrier \nand promise to send an update before eleven thirty.\n\nThao’s message is polite, clear and specific. \nShe **focuses on moving the work forward instead of blaming the supplier.****\n\n**NEW VOCABULARY**\n\n| Word or phrase        | Meaning in Vietnamese              |\n| --------------------- | ---------------------------------- |\n| follow up             | theo dõi và hỏi lại về một việc    |\n| confirmation          | sự xác nhận                        |\n| pending               | đang chờ xử lý                     |\n| current status        | trạng thái hiện tại                |\n| reminder              | lời nhắc                           |\n| delayed response      | phản hồi bị chậm                   |\n| carrier               | đơn vị vận chuyển                  |\n| final approval        | phê duyệt cuối cùng                |\n| create tension        | tạo ra sự căng thẳng               |\n| specific              | cụ thể                             |\n| response time         | thời gian phản hồi                 |\n| move the work forward | giúp công việc tiếp tục tiến triển |\n\n  \n  \n\n**MULTIPLE CHOICE QUESTIONS**\n\n1. What is Thao waiting for?\n\nA. A payment report  \n**B. A shipment date confirmation**  \nC. A new invoice  \nD. A system update\n\n2. Which teams need the information?\n\nA. IT and Accounting  \n**B. Warehouse and Production**  \nC. HR and Purchasing  \nD. Finance and Inspection\n \n\n3. Why does Thao change her first message?\n\nA. It is too polite.  \nB. It has the wrong PO number.  \n**C. It sounds angry and does not explain the reason.**  \nD. It is too long.\n\n4. Why has the supplier not confirmed the shipment date?\n\nA. The invoice is incorrect.  \n**B. They are waiting for the carrier’s final approval.**  \nC. The warehouse is full.  \nD. Production changed the order.\n\n5. What makes Thao’s **second message** professional?\n\nA. She blames the supplier clearly.  \n**B. She explains the reason and asks for a response time.**  \nC. She sends the same message many times.  \nD. She removes all deadlines.  \n\n2. **TONE LADDER** \n\n**LEVEL 1: PUSHY OR BAD**\n\nThese sentences may sound blaming, impatient or demanding.\n\n- Why haven’t you replied yet?\n\n- Send me the confirmation now.\n\n- You promised to send it yesterday.\n\n- We have waited too long.\n\n*Why do they sound bad?*\n\n- They focus on the person’s failure.\n\n- They use commands.\n\n- They do not explain the **business reason**.\n\n- They may make the other person defensive.\n\n**LEVEL 2: POLITE**\n\n- ***Do you have any update*** on the **shipment date**?\n\n- ***Could you please check*** the current status?\n\n- **Could you let me know when** the confirmation will be available?\n\nThese sentences are respectful, but they are still clear.\n\n**LEVEL 3: COLLABORATIVE**\n\nI’m following up on the **shipment date** confirmation for **PO 4582**.\n\nOur Warehouse team needs the information to **prepare for the delivery**.\n\nCould you please update us by **eleven thirty today**?\n\nPlease let me know if **you need any information** from our side.\n\n**COLLABORATIVE FOLLOW UP FLOW**\n\n**Previous request**\n\n→ Current status\n\n→ Business reason\n\n→ Response time\n\n→ Offer support\n\n→ Polite closing\n\n**COMPLETE MODEL**\n\nGood morning, Mr. Lee. I’m following up on the shipment date confirmation for PO 4582. Our Warehouse team needs the information to prepare for the delivery. Could you please update us by eleven thirty today? Please let me know if you need any information from our side. Thank you for your support.\n\n3. EXPRESSION BANK\n\n**FUNCTION A: OPEN THE FOLLOW UP**\n\n| Complete expression                                    | Meaning in Vietnamese                       | Connected speech rhythm                     | PPJ workplace example                 | Rapid fire trigger        |\n| ------------------------------------------------------ | ------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------- | ------------------------- |\n| ***I’m following up on*** **my previous message.**     | Tôi đang theo dõi lại tin nhắn trước.       | I’m FOLlowing up / on my previous message.  | Supplier chưa phản hồi email hôm qua. | Theo dõi **tin nhắn cũ.** |\n| ***I’m following up on*** the **document request**.    | Tôi đang theo dõi **yêu cầu gửi chứng từ**. | I’m FOLlowing up / on the document request. | Packing list vẫn chưa được gửi.       | Nhắc **chứng từ.**        |\n| ***Just checking in on***  + Obj(the shipment update.) | Tôi đang hỏi lại về cập nhật lô hàng.       | Just CHECKing in / on the shipment update.  | Chưa nhận được ETA mới.               | Hỏi shipment.             |\n\n**FUNCTION B: ASK FOR THE CURRENT STATUS**\n\n| Complete expression                                  | Meaning in Vietnamese                                  | Connected speech rhythm                         | PPJ workplace example                  | Rapid fire trigger      |\n| ---------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------ | ----------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------- | ----------------------- |\n| **Do you have any update on** + Object (this issue)? | Bạn có cập nhật gì về vấn đề này không?                | Do you have any UPdate / on this issue?         | Quantity mismatch vẫn chưa được xử lý. | Có update chưa?         |\n| **Could you update me on** the current status?       | Bạn có thể cập nhật trạng thái hiện tại cho tôi không? | Could you UPdate me / on the current status?    | Cần biết payment đang ở bước nào.      | Xin status.             |\n| Has the supplier confirmed the delivery date?        | Supplier đã xác nhận ngày giao hàng chưa?              | Has the supplier conFIRMED / the delivery date? | Warehouse đang chờ ngày nhận hàng.     | Supplier xác nhận chưa? |\n\n  \n\n**FUNCTION C: EXPLAIN THE BUSINESS REASON**\n\n| Complete expression                                            | Meaning in Vietnamese                                     | Connected speech rhythm                                        | PPJ workplace example             | Rapid fire trigger  |\n| -------------------------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------- | ------------------- |\n| **We need the** confirmation **to** update our internal teams. | Chúng tôi cần xác nhận để cập nhật các team nội bộ.       | We need the confirMAtion / to update our internal teams.       | XNK cần cập nhật Kho và Sản xuất. | Nêu lý do nội bộ.   |\n| The warehouse is waiting for the delivery details.             | Kho đang chờ thông tin giao hàng.                         | The WAREhouse is waiting / for the delivery details.           | Kho cần chuẩn bị nhận hàng.       | Kho đang chờ.       |\n| We need this information to complete the shipment documents.   | Chúng tôi cần thông tin này để hoàn tất chứng từ lô hàng. | We need this inforMAtion / to complete the shipment documents. | Hồ sơ shipment chưa thể hoàn tất. | Cần hoàn tất hồ sơ. |\n\n**FUNCTION D: REQUEST A RESPONSE AND CLOSE**\n\n| Complete expression                                           | Meaning in Vietnamese                                     | Connected speech rhythm                                           | PPJ workplace example               | Rapid fire trigger   |\n| ------------------------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------- | -------------------- |\n| **Could you please update** us **by** noon today?             | Bạn có thể cập nhật trước trưa nay không?                 | Could you please UPdate us / by noon today?                       | Cần thông tin trước cuộc họp chiều. | Xin trước trưa.      |\n| When can we expect your confirmation?                         | Khi nào chúng tôi có thể nhận được xác nhận?              | When can we exPECT / your confirmation?                           | Supplier chưa cung cấp timeline.    | Khi nào có xác nhận? |\n| Please let me know if you need any information from our side. | Hãy cho tôi biết nếu bạn cần thông tin từ phía chúng tôi. | Please let me KNOW / if you need any information / from our side. | PPJ sẵn sàng bổ sung dữ liệu.       | Cần gì từ PPJ?       |\n\nRAPID FIRE PRACTICE\n\nBạn nghe trigger tiếng Việt và trả lời trong 5 giây.\n\n|Trigger|Complete response|\n|---|---|\n|Theo dõi tin nhắn cũ|I’m following up on my previous message.|\n|Nhắc chứng từ|I’m following up on the document request.|\n|Có update chưa?|Do you have any update on this issue?|\n|Xin status|Could you update me on the current status?|\n|Supplier xác nhận chưa?|Has the supplier confirmed the delivery date?|\n|Kho đang chờ|The warehouse is waiting for the delivery details.|\n|Cần hoàn tất hồ sơ|We need this information to complete the shipment documents.|\n|Xin trước trưa|Could you please update us by noon today?|\n|Khi nào có xác nhận?|When can we expect your confirmation?|\n|Cần gì từ PPJ?|Please let me know if you need any information from our side.|\n\n  \n\n4. THREE SPEAKING PATTERNS\n\nPATTERN 1: SIMPLE STATUS FOLLOW UP\n\nFramework\n\nI’m following up on + pending task.\n\n  \n\nDo you have any update on + specific information?\n\nSample answer\n\nI’m following up on the revised packing list. Do you have any update on the document status?\n\nYour answer\n\n|Part|Your information|\n|---|---|\n|Pending task||\n|Information you need||\n\nSay your complete follow up to your partner.\n\nPATTERN 2: FOLLOW UP WITH A REASON AND RESPONSE TIME\n\nFramework\n\nI’m following up on + pending task.\n\n  \n\nWe need the information to + business reason.\n\n  \n\nCould you please update us by + time?\n\nSample answer\n\nI’m following up on the shipment date confirmation. We need the information to update the Warehouse team. Could you please update us by noon today?\n\nYour answer\n\n|Part|Your information|\n|---|---|\n|Pending task||\n|Business reason||\n|Response time||\n\nSay your complete follow up twice.\n\nRound 1: Use your notes.\n\nRound 2: Speak without reading the full sentence.\n\nPATTERN 3: COMPLETE COLLABORATIVE FOLLOW UP\n\nFramework\n\nGood morning, + name.\n\n  \n\nI’m following up on + pending task.\n\n  \n\nWe need it because + business reason.\n\n  \n\nWhen can we expect + required information?\n\n  \n\nPlease let me know if you need + support or information.\n\n  \n\nThank you for your support.\n\nSample answer\n\nGood morning, Mr. Lee. I’m following up on the delivery date confirmation for PO 4582. We need it because Warehouse is preparing to receive the goods. When can we expect the final confirmation? Please let me know if you need any information from our side. Thank you for your support.\n\nYour answer\n\n|Part|Your information|\n|---|---|\n|Person’s name||\n|Pending task||\n|Business reason||\n|Information you need||\n|Support you can offer||\n\nGive your complete message to a partner.\n\nYour partner must answer:\n\nWe are still working on it.\n\nContinue the conversation and ask for a response time.\n\n5. **TRANSFORM THE SENTENCES**\n\nChange each pushy, unclear or incomplete sentence into a polite and collaborative follow up.\n\n| Pushy or unclear sentence              | Your professional version                                                                                                                                       |\n| -------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Send me the confirmation now.          | I'm following up on + the confirmation that we talked about last week. Do you have any update on it? Please let me know if you need any additional information. |\n| Why are you not replying?              |                                                                                                                                                                 |\n| Any update?                            |                                                                                                                                                                 |\n| We are still waiting.                  |                                                                                                                                                                 |\n| You promised to send it yesterday.     |                                                                                                                                                                 |\n| Tell me when the shipment will arrive. |                                                                                                                                                                 |\n| Reply before lunch.                    |                                                                                                                                                                 |\n| The warehouse needs this now.          |                                                                                                                                                                 |\n| Check the payment status again.        |                                                                                                                                                                 |\n| This is urgent. Do it immediately.     |                                                                                                                                                                 |\n\nUSE THIS CHECKLIST\n\nA strong transformation should include at least three elements.\n\n□ What you are following up on  \n□ Why the information is needed  \n□ When you need the response  \n□ A polite question  \n□ An offer of support  \n□ A professional closing\n\nMODEL TRANSFORMATION\n\nPushy\n\nWhy haven’t you sent the file?\n\nCollaborative\n\nI’m following up on the file request from yesterday. We need the file to complete the shipment documents. Could you please let me know when it will be available?\n\n6. ROLEPLAY: MISSING SUPPLIER CONFIRMATION\n\nSITUATION\n\nPPJ is waiting for a supplier to confirm the shipment date for PO 4582.\n\n|Information|Details|\n|---|---|\n|Expected confirmation|Tuesday afternoon|\n|Current time|Wednesday at 9:30 a.m.|\n|Warehouse deadline|Before noon|\n|Internal impact|Production also needs the shipment date|\n\n  \n  \n\nROLE A: PPJ IMPORT EXPORT\n\nYou need to:\n\n1. Mention the previous request.\n    \n2. Ask for the current status.\n    \n3. Explain why PPJ needs the information.\n    \n4. Ask for a clear response time.\n    \n5. Offer support.\n    \n6. Close politely.\n    \n\nUse at least five sentences.\n\nI’m following up on my previous message.\n\n  \n\nDo you have any update on the shipment date?\n\n  \n\nThe warehouse is waiting for the delivery details.\n\n  \n\nCould you please update us by noon today?\n\n  \n\nWhen can we expect your confirmation?\n\n  \n\nPlease let me know if you need any information from our side.\n\n  \n\nThank you for your support.\n\n  \n  \n\nROLE B: SUPPLIER\n\nYour situation:\n\n|Information|Details|\n|---|---|\n|Current problem|The carrier has not given final approval.|\n|Expected carrier response|11:00 a.m.|\n|Your update to PPJ|11:30 a.m.|\n|Information you need|Confirmation of the delivery address|\n\nUse at least four sentences.\n\nI’m sorry for the delayed response.\n\n  \n\nWe’re still waiting for final approval from the carrier.\n\n  \n\nWe expect an update at eleven o’clock.\n\n  \n\nI can send you the confirmation by eleven thirty.\n\n  \n\nCould you confirm the delivery address?\n\n  \n\nThank you for your patience.  \n\nCONVERSATION FLOW\n\nGreeting\n\n→ Previous request\n\n→ Current status question\n\n→ Reason for the delay\n\n→ Business impact\n\n→ Response time → Information needed → Next action → Polite closing\n\nFINAL AGREEMENT\n\nComplete the summary together.\n\n|Item|Agreed information|\n|---|---|\n|The supplier is waiting for||\n|The supplier will update PPJ by||\n|PPJ will send||\n|Warehouse and Production will be updated after||\n\nSUCCESS CHECK\n\n□ I mentioned the previous request.  \n□ I asked for a clear update.  \n□ I explained why the information was needed.  \n□ I asked for a realistic response time.  \n□ I avoided blaming language.  \n□ I offered support or information.  \n□ We agreed on the next action.  \n□ I closed politely.  \n\n7. SESSION TAKEAWAY\n\nA professional follow up does not focus on:\n\nWhy haven’t you replied?\n\nIt focuses on:\n\nWhat is pending?\n\n+\n\nWhy is the information needed?\n\n+\n\nWhen is the update required?\n\n+\n\nWhat is the next action?\n\nFIVE SENTENCES TO REMEMBER\n\nI’m following up on my previous message.\n\nDo you have any update on this issue?\n\nWe need the information to update our internal teams.\n\nCould you please update us by noon today?\n\nPlease let me know if you need any information from our side.  \n\nFINAL MESSAGE\n\nFollow up on the work without attacking the person.\n\nNội dung chủ đề và đầu ra được phát triển theo Session 7 trong course master plan.\n\n  \n**","x":9080,"y":10800,"width":920,"height":18243},
		{"id":"1068ec55f2d21ba8","type":"text","text":"# STUDENT WORKSHEET\n\n## TENSES FOR REAL WORKPLACE COMMUNICATION\n\nLevel: A2–B1  \nClass time: 75 minutes + 15 minutes Kahoot  \nFocus: Use tenses to talk about routines, current situations, past events, experience, ongoing past actions, and future plans.\n\n### Learning Goals\n\nBy the end of this lesson, you can:\n\n- describe what you usually do\n    \n- explain what is happening now\n    \n- report what happened\n    \n- talk about an action that was happening\n    \n- explain what has happened / has changed\n    \n- talk about future plans, decisions, and predictions\n    \n\n## 1. THE BIG PICTURE: 6 TENSES YOU REALLY NEED\n\n|   |   |   |   |\n|---|---|---|---|\n|Tense|Main Idea|Common Time Markers|Workplace Example|\n|Present Simple|routine / fact|usually, every day, often, normally|I check invoices every morning.|\n|Present Continuous|happening now / temporary|now, right now, currently, this week|I’m checking a supplier invoice now.|\n|Past Simple|finished past event|yesterday, last week, this morning|We received the documents yesterday.|\n|Past Continuous|action in progress in the past|while, when, at 3 p.m. yesterday|I was checking the report when she called.|\n|Present Perfect|past + connection to now|already, yet, just, ever, never, since, for|We have received the revised file.|\n|Future|plan / decision / prediction|tomorrow, next week, later|I’ll contact the supplier later.|\n\n### The Core Question\n\nBefore choosing a tense, ask:\n\nWhat TIME am I talking about, and how is the action connected to that time?\n\n## 2. READING: ONE BUSY DAY AT WORK\n\nRead the story. Underline the verbs.\n\n### A Busy Morning\n\nLinh works in the Purchasing Department. She usually checks supplier confirmations before 9 a.m.\n\nToday is different. She is dealing with an urgent material issue because Production needs the fabric tomorrow.\n\nYesterday, the supplier sent a confirmation, but the quantity was incorrect. Linh was reviewing another purchase order when the warehouse called her about the problem.\n\nShe has already contacted the supplier and has received a revised confirmation. However, the supplier has not confirmed the delivery time yet.\n\nLinh says:\n\n“I’ll follow up with them again this afternoon. We’re going to update Production as soon as we receive the final confirmation.”\n\n  \n  \n\n### Check Your Understanding\n\nChoose the best answer.\n\n1. What does Linh normally do before 9 a.m.?\n\nA. Contact Production  \nB. Check supplier confirmations  \nC. Receive materials  \nD. Prepare invoices\n\n2. Why is today different?\n\nA. She is on leave.  \nB. The warehouse is closed.  \nC. She is handling an urgent material issue.  \nD. The supplier cancelled the order.\n\n3. What happened yesterday?\n\nA. The supplier sent an incorrect confirmation.  \nB. Production changed the order.  \nC. Linh received the fabric.  \nD. The warehouse rejected the materials.\n\n4. What was Linh doing when the warehouse called?\n\nA. Contacting the supplier  \nB. Reviewing another PO  \nC. Updating Production  \nD. Checking an invoice\n\n5. What has Linh already done?\n\nA. Received the materials  \nB. Cancelled the PO  \nC. Contacted the supplier  \nD. Confirmed the delivery time\n\n6. What will she do later?\n\nA. Follow up with the supplier  \nB. Create another PO  \nC. Visit the warehouse  \nD. Cancel the shipment\n\n## 3. TENSE 1: PRESENT SIMPLE\n\n### Use it for\n\nRoutine\n\nI check the stock every morning.\n\nRegular responsibility\n\nFinance prepares the monthly report.\n\nFact\n\nOur warehouse stores finished goods.\n\n### Pattern\n\nI / You / We / They + verb\n\nWe check the data.\n\nHe / She / It + verb-s\n\nShe checks the data.\n\n### Workplace Triggers\n\n- every day\n    \n- usually\n    \n- normally\n    \n- often\n    \n- sometimes\n    \n- always\n    \n- once a week\n    \n\n### Your Turn\n\nComplete the sentences.\n\n1. I usually __________ before I start work.\n    \n2. My department normally __________.\n    \n3. We often work with __________.\n    \n4. I sometimes need English when __________.\n    \n\n## 4. TENSE 2: PRESENT CONTINUOUS\n\n### Use it for\n\nSomething happening now\n\nI’m checking the invoice right now.\n\nA temporary situation\n\nWe’re working on a new project this month.\n\n### Pattern\n\nam / is / are + verb-ing\n\nI’m checking it.\n\nShe’s talking to the supplier.\n\nThey’re preparing the shipment.\n\n### Compare\n\nRoutine\n\nI check invoices every morning.\n\nRight now\n\nI’m checking an invoice now.\n\n### Your Turn\n\nWhat is happening in your department right now / this week?\n\n5. I’m currently ______________________________.\n    \n6. My team is _________________________________.\n    \n7. We’re working on ___________________________.\n    \n\n## 5. TENSE 3: PAST SIMPLE\n\n### Use it for\n\nA finished action in the past.\n\nThe supplier sent the document yesterday.\n\nWe found an error this morning.\n\nProduction changed the quantity last week.\n\n### Common Signals\n\n- yesterday\n    \n- last week\n    \n- two days ago\n    \n- this morning\n    \n- in July\n    \n- at 10 a.m.\n    \n\n### Regular vs Irregular\n\n|   |   |\n|---|---|\n|Present|Past|\n|check|checked|\n|receive|received|\n|contact|contacted|\n|send|sent|\n|find|found|\n|make|made|\n|have|had|\n|go|went|\n\n### Complete Your Story\n\nThink about one problem you handled recently.\n\nYesterday / Last week, __________________________________.\n\nThe problem was ________________________________________.\n\nI contacted _____________________________________________.\n\nWe decided to ___________________________________________.\n\nFinally, ________________________________________________.\n\n## 6. TENSE 4: PAST CONTINUOUS\n\n### Use it for\n\nAn action that was already happening when another event occurred.\n\nI was checking the report when my manager called.\n\n### Pattern\n\nwas / were + verb-ing\n\n### Useful Speaking Pattern\n\nI was ___ing when ___ happened.\n\nExamples:\n\nI was preparing the report when the system stopped working.\n\nWe were discussing the shipment when the supplier called.\n\n### Your Turn\n\nComplete naturally.\n\n1. I was working on __________ when __________.\n    \n2. We were checking __________ when __________.\n    \n3. The team was discussing __________ when __________.\n    \n\n## 7. TENSE 5: PRESENT PERFECT\n\nThis is one of the most useful tenses in workplace English.\n\n### Use it when\n\nSomething happened before and the result matters NOW.\n\nWe have received the document.\n\nMeaning:\n\nThe document is available now.\n\n### Pattern\n\nhave / has + past participle\n\nI have sent it.\n\nShe has checked it.\n\nThey have finished the report.\n\n### High-Frequency Workplace Expressions\n\n|   |   |\n|---|---|\n|Expression|Meaning|\n|I’ve sent it.|Tôi gửi rồi.|\n|We’ve received it.|Bên tôi nhận được rồi.|\n|I’ve already checked it.|Tôi kiểm tra rồi.|\n|We haven’t received it yet.|Bên tôi vẫn chưa nhận được.|\n|Has the supplier confirmed it yet?|NCC xác nhận chưa?|\n|There has been a change.|Đã có thay đổi.|\n|We’ve found an issue.|Bên tôi phát hiện một vấn đề.|\n\n### Past Simple vs Present Perfect\n\nPast Simple = WHEN matters\n\nI sent it yesterday.\n\nPresent Perfect = RESULT NOW matters\n\nI’ve sent it.\n\nCompare:\n\nDid you send the report yesterday?\n\nHave you sent the report yet?\n\n## 8. TENSE 6: TALKING ABOUT THE FUTURE\n\n### WILL\n\nUse for:\n\nDecision now\n\nI’ll check it.\n\nPromise\n\nI’ll send you an update.\n\nPrediction\n\nI think the shipment will arrive tomorrow.\n\n### BE GOING TO\n\nUse for an existing intention or plan.\n\nWe’re going to review the report tomorrow.\n\n### PRESENT CONTINUOUS FOR ARRANGEMENTS\n\nUse when something is already arranged.\n\nI’m meeting the supplier at 2 p.m.\n\n### Compare\n\nSituation:\n\nYour colleague asks:\n\n“Can you check this file?”\n\nYou decide now:\n\nSure, I’ll check it.\n\nYou already planned it:\n\nI’m going to check it this afternoon.\n\nYou already have a scheduled review:\n\nI’m reviewing it with Finance at 3 p.m.\n\n## 9. CHOOSE THE TENSE\n\nChoose the best answer.\n\n### 1\n\nI usually _____ the inventory report every Monday.\n\n**A. prepare**  \nB. am preparing  \nC. prepared  \nD. have prepared\n\n### 2\n\nPlease give me five minutes. I am checking_____ the information **now.**\n\nA. check  \nB. checked  \nC. am checking  \nD. have checked\n\n### 3\n\nThe supplier sent the revised invoice yesterday.\n\nA. sends  \n**B. sent**  \nC. has sent  \nD. is sending\n\n### 4\n\nI _____ the report when **my manager called me.** \n\n**A. had prepared** - da hoan tat truoc khi manager called me  \nB. have prepared  \n**C. was preparing**  - muon nhan manh viec dang chuan bi trong khi manager called me\nD. prepare\n\n### 5\n\nWe _____ the documents **yet** ///// **yet + perfect**\n\nA. didn't receive  \n**B. haven't received**  \nC. don't receive  \nD. aren't receiving\n\n### 6\n\nDon’t worry. I _____ the warehouse.\n\nA. contact  \nB. contacted  \n**C. have contacted**  --- nhan manh vao viec khong phai lo - **vi moi thu da em xuoi, hoan thanh roi**\n**D. will contact** --- nhan manh vao viec khong phai lo - **vi toi se handle viec do**\n\n  \n  \n\n### 7\n\nShe _____ in Purchasing **for three years**. /////\n- *for + time* --> perfect: **she has worked in PUR for 3 years**\n- *since + time* --> perfect: **she has worked in PUR since 2022**\n\nA. works  \nB. worked  \n**C. has worked**  \nD. is working yesterday\n\n### 8\n\nWe _____ with Finance at 3 p.m. tomorrow. The meeting is already scheduled.\n\nA. met  \n**B. \nare meeting**  - 100%  \nbe going to -> 6-70%\nwill -> 50%\nC. meet yesterday  \nD. have met\n\n## 10. TENSE TRANSFORMATION\n\nChange the sentence according to the situation.\n\n### Example\n\n**Routine:**  \nI check supplier confirmations.\n\n**Now:**  \n*I’m checking* a supplier confirmation.\n\n### A\n\n**Routine**\n\nWe check the inventory every morning.\n\n**Right now**\n\n  \n\n### B\n\nNow\n\nI’m discussing the issue with Production.\n\nYesterday\n\n  \n\n### C\n\nFinished yesterday\n\nThe supplier sent the invoice yesterday.\n\nResult matters now\n\n  \n\n### D\n\nAction in progress\n\nI was reviewing the PO.\n\nAdd interruption:\n\n__________________________________ when __________.\n\n### E\n\nProblem now\n\nWe haven’t received the document yet.\n\nMake a future action:\n\n  \n\n## 11. THREE SPEAKING PATTERNS\n\n### Pattern 1: ROUTINE → CURRENT SITUATION\n\nNormally, I __________, but today I’m __________.\n\nExample:\n\nNormally, I prepare regular reports, but today I’m working on an urgent request.\n\nYour sentence:\n\nNormally, I ____________________, but today ____________________.\n\n### Pattern 2: PAST EVENT → CURRENT RESULT\n\nYesterday, __________. So now, we’ve __________.\n\nExample:\n\nYesterday, we found a quantity mismatch. So now, we’ve asked the supplier to revise the document.\n\nYour sentence:\n\nYesterday, ____________________. So now, ____________________.\n\n### Pattern 3: PROBLEM → FUTURE ACTION\n\nWe haven’t __________ yet, so I’ll __________.\n\nExample:\n\nWe haven’t received confirmation yet, so I’ll follow up this afternoon.\n\nYour sentence:\n\nWe haven’t ____________________, so I’ll ____________________.\n\n  \n  \n  \n\n## 12. RAPID-FIRE TENSE CHALLENGE\n\nYour partner gives you a time trigger. Say one workplace sentence immediately.\n\n|   |   |\n|---|---|\n|Trigger|Use|\n|Every Monday|Present Simple|\n|Right now|Present Continuous|\n|Yesterday|Past Simple|\n|When my manager called|Past Continuous|\n|Already|Present Perfect|\n|Yet|Present Perfect|\n|Tomorrow|Future|\n|Next week|Future|\n|For three years|Present Perfect|\n|Last year|Past Simple|\n\n### Example\n\nPartner: “Right now!”\n\nYou:\n\nI’m checking the monthly report right now.\n\n## 13. FIND AND FIX THE MISTAKE\n\nCorrect each sentence.\n\n4. I am usually check the report every morning.  \n    → ___________________________________________\n    \n5. Yesterday I have contacted the supplier.  \n    → ___________________________________________\n    \n6. I working on the invoice now.  \n    → ___________________________________________\n    \n7. We haven't received the documents yesterday.  \n    → ___________________________________________\n    \n8. I was check the PO when she called.  \n    → ___________________________________________\n    \n9. The supplier hasn't reply yet.  \n    → ___________________________________________\n    \n10. Tomorrow I contacted the warehouse.  \n    → ___________________________________________\n    \n11. She work here for five years.  \n    → ___________________________________________\n    \n\n  \n  \n  \n\n## 14. PAIR ROLEPLAY: THE MISSING MATERIAL CONFIRMATION\n\n### Situation\n\nProduction needs material urgently.\n\nThe supplier sent some information yesterday, but the final delivery confirmation has not arrived.\n\n### Student A: Purchasing\n\nExplain:\n\n- what you normally do\n    \n- what happened yesterday\n    \n- what you are doing now\n    \n- what you have already done\n    \n- what has not happened yet\n    \n- what you will do next\n    \n\n### Student B: Production\n\nAsk:\n\n- What happened?\n    \n- When did you contact the supplier?\n    \n- Have they replied yet?\n    \n- What are you doing now?\n    \n- What will you do next?\n    \n- When can Production expect an update?\n    \n\n### Useful Expressions\n\nWe usually…\n\nYesterday, we…\n\nI was … when…\n\nWe’ve already…\n\nWe haven’t … yet.\n\nI’m currently…\n\nI’ll…\n\nWe’re going to…\n\n### Challenge\n\nUse at least five different tenses/forms during the conversation.\n\n  \n  \n\n## 15. SPEAKING: MY WORK TIMELINE\n\nTalk to a partner.\n\n### Routine\n\nWhat do you normally do at work?\n\nI usually…\n\n### Now\n\nWhat are you currently working on?\n\nRight now, I’m…\n\n### Recent Past\n\nWhat did you do yesterday?\n\nYesterday, I…\n\n### Interrupted Action\n\nWhat were you doing when someone contacted you recently?\n\nI was… when…\n\n### Recent Result\n\nWhat have you completed recently?\n\nI’ve already…\n\n### Unfinished Task\n\nWhat haven’t you finished yet?\n\nI haven’t… yet.\n\n### Future\n\nWhat will you do after class / tomorrow?\n\nI’ll…\n\n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n\n## 16. FINAL CHALLENGE: TELL ONE COMPLETE WORK STORY\n\nSpeak for 60–90 seconds.\n\nYour story must include:\n\n1. Present Simple — your normal responsibility\n    \n2. Present Continuous — what is happening now\n    \n3. Past Simple — what happened\n    \n4. Past Continuous — what was happening\n    \n5. Present Perfect — current result\n    \n6. Future — next action\n    \n\n### Story Frame\n\nNormally, I __________________________.\n\nToday, I’m __________________________.\n\nYesterday, __________________________.\n\nI was __________________ when __________________.\n\nWe’ve already _______________________.\n\nHowever, we haven’t __________________ yet.\n\nNext, I’ll ___________________________.\n\n## TAKEAWAY\n\nDon't ask:\n\n“Which tense should I use?”\n\nAsk:\n\n“What time am I talking about?”\n\n### Remember\n\nRoutine → Present Simple  \nNow → Present Continuous  \nFinished past → Past Simple  \nIn progress in the past → Past Continuous  \nPast + result now → Present Perfect  \nNext action / plan → Future\n\nThe goal is not to memorize 12 tense formulas.\n\nThe goal is to move naturally between past → present → future when telling a real story.\n\n  \n**","x":10752,"y":9183,"width":788,"height":23234},
		{"id":"d4d9a80491192d29","type":"text","text":"homework 3: hoan thanh transformation of sentences","x":8900,"y":18060,"width":173,"height":120},
		{"id":"412de5ac4aebd360","type":"text","text":"## Phân biệt Simple Past và Present Perfect\n\n### 1. Simple Past = việc đã kết thúc trong quá khứ\n\n**Cấu trúc:**\n\n> S + V2/V-ed\n\nDùng khi **thời điểm trong quá khứ đã rõ hoặc đã kết thúc**.\n\n**Ví dụ:**\n\n- I **sent** the email **yesterday.**\n    \n- She **worked** at PPJ **in 2024.**\n    \n- We **met** the supplier **last Monday.**\n    \n- I **visited** Thailand **three years ago.**\n    \n\nTừ khóa thường gặp:\n\n- yesterday\n    \n- last week / last year\n    \n- two days ago\n    \n- in 2025\n    \n- when I was younger\n    \n\n### 2. Present Perfect = quá khứ có liên hệ với hiện tại\n\n**Cấu trúc:**\n\n> S + have/has + V3\n\nKhông tập trung vào **khi nào**, mà tập trung vào **kết quả, trải nghiệm hoặc khoảng thời gian kéo dài đến hiện tại**.\n\n**Ví dụ:**\n\n- I **have sent** the email.  \n    → Email đã được gửi rồi, **đó là tình trạng hiện tại**.\n    \n- She **has worked** at PPJ for five years.  \n    → Cô ấy bắt đầu 5 năm trước và **vẫn đang làm ở PPJ**.\n    - she hás worked at PPJ **since** 2024\n    \n- We **have met** this supplier several times.  \n    → Đây là trải nghiệm tính đến hiện tại.\n    \n- I **have visited** Thailand three times.  \n    → Không quan trọng chính xác khi nào.\n    \n\n### 3. So sánh trực tiếp\n\n|Simple Past|Present Perfect|\n|---|---|\n|Quá khứ đã kết thúc|Quá khứ liên quan đến hiện tại|\n|Có thời điểm quá khứ rõ|Thường không nói thời điểm cụ thể|\n|**When did it happen?**|**Has it happened yet / ever?**|\n|V2|have/has + V3|\n\nVí dụ rất quan trọng:\n\n**I worked at PPJ for five years.**  \n→ Tôi đã làm PPJ 5 năm, **nhưng bây giờ không còn làm nữa**.\n\n**I have worked at PPJ for five years.**  \n→ Tôi đã làm PPJ được 5 năm và **hiện vẫn đang làm**.\n\n### 4. Một cặp câu rất dễ nhầm\n\n**Did you send the invoice yesterday?**  \n→ Hỏi về **yesterday**, nên Simple Past.\n\n**Have you sent the invoice yet?**  \n→ Hỏi xem **đến hiện tại đã gửi chưa**, nên Present Perfect.\n\nTương tự:\n\n- When **did you receive** the document?\n    \n- **Have you received** the document yet?\n    \n\n### 5. Mẹo nhớ nhanh\n\nHãy tự hỏi:\n\n> **Có nói rõ WHEN trong một khoảng thời gian đã kết thúc không?**\n\nCó → **Simple Past**\n\n> I called him **this morning**.  \n> _(nếu buổi sáng đã kết thúc)_\n\nKhông, chỉ quan tâm **đến hiện tại đã xảy ra chưa / bao nhiêu lần / kéo dài bao lâu** → **Present Perfect**\n\n> I have called him three times / today.\n\n### 6. Những từ thường đi với Present Perfect\n\n- already\n    \n- yet\n    \n- just\n    \n- ever // have you ever + ....\n    \n- never\n    \n- recently\n    \n- so far\n    \n- for + time\n    \n- since + time in the past\n    \n- several times\n    \n\nVí dụ:\n\n- I’ve **already checked** the report.\n    \n- Have you **finished yet**?\n    \n- We’ve **just received** the confirmation.\n    \n- I’ve **never worked** with this supplier before.\n    \n- She has worked here **since 2022**.\n    \n- She has worked here **for four years**.\n    \n\n### Reflex cần nhớ\n\n> **Past Simple = WHEN?**  \n> **Present Perfect = UP TO NOW?**\n\nVí dụ workplace:\n\n**When did the supplier reply?**  \n→ Yesterday. → Past Simple\n\n**Has the supplier replied yet?**  \n→ Yes, they have. → Present Perfect","x":11920,"y":5800,"width":720,"height":2960},
		{"id":"9cce903663d539c4","type":"text","text":"# Bài tập: Present Simple → Past Simple → Present Perfect\n\n### Cách làm\n\nMỗi câu ban đầu ở **Present Simple**. Viết lại thành:\n\n1. **Past Simple** theo ngữ cảnh quá khứ đã kết thúc.\n    \n2. **Present Perfect** theo ngữ cảnh có liên hệ đến hiện tại.\n    \n\n### Example\n\n**Present Simple:**  \nI check the invoice every morning.\n\n**Past context:** yesterday morning  \n→ I **checked the invoice yesterday morning**.\n\n**Present Perfect context:** already / today  \n→ I **have already checked the invoice today**.\n\n## Exercise\n\n### 1\n\n**Present Simple:**  \nI send reports to my manager every Friday.\n\n**Past context:** last Friday  \n→ ____________________________________________I sent reports to my manager last Friday\n\n**Present Perfect context:** already / this week  \n→ ____________________________________________I have already sent the reports to my manager\n\n### 2\n\n**Present Simple:**  \nShe -  **checks** - the supplier documents -  **carefully**. - careful -> adv:: \n>\n\n**Past context:** yesterday  \n→ ____________________________________________ she checked the supplier documents carefully yesterday\n\n**Present Perfect context:** all the documents / already  \n→ ____________________________________________she has already checked all the documents carefully \nshe has checked all the documents carefully already \n\n### 3\n\n**Present Simple:**  \nWe receive many requests from the warehouse.\n\n**Past context:** yesterday morning  \n→ ____________________________________________\n\n**Present Perfect context:** three requests / so far today  \n→ ____________________________________________\n\n### 4\n\n**Present Simple:**  \nHe works with the Purchasing Department.\n\n**Past context:** in 2024  \n→ ____________________________________________\n\n**Present Perfect context:** for two years / still works there  \n→ ____________________________________________\n\n\n### 5\n\n**Present Simple:**  \nThey meet the supplier every month.\n\n**Past context:** last Monday  \n→ ____________________________________________\n\n**Present Perfect context:** twice / this month  \n→\n\n### 6\n\n**Present Simple:**  \nI finish my tasks before 5 p.m.\n\n**Past context:** yesterday  \n→ \n\n**Present Perfect context:** all my tasks / already  \n→ ____________________________________________\n\n### 7\n\n**Present Simple:**  \nThe supplier sends us the packing list.\n\n**Past context:** two days ago  \n→ \n\n**Present Perfect context:** just  \n→ ____________________________________________\n\n### 8\n\n**Present Simple:**  \nWe find mistakes in the invoice **sometimes**. / find -> found -> found\n\n**Past context:** during yesterday's check  \n→ ____________________________________________*\n\n**Present Perfect context:** two mistakes / so far  \n→ ____________________________________________\n\n### 9\n\n**Present Simple:**  \nShe is responsible for this report.\n\n**Past context:** last year  \n→ \n\n**Present Perfect context:** since January  \n→ ____________________________________________\n\n### 10\n\n**Present Simple:**  \nI talk to the production team about this problem.\n\n**Past context:** this morning  \n→ \n\n**Present Perfect context:** several times / this week  \n→\n\n# Answer Key\n\n|#|Past Simple|Present Perfect|\n|---|---|---|\n|1|I **sent** the report to my manager last Friday.|I **have already sent** the report this week.|\n|2|She **checked** the supplier documents yesterday.|She **has already checked** all the documents.|\n|3|We **received** many requests from the warehouse yesterday morning.|We **have received** three requests so far today.|\n|4|He **worked** with the Purchasing Department in 2024.|He **has worked** with the Purchasing Department for two years.|\n|5|They **met** the supplier last Monday.|They **have met** the supplier twice this month.|\n|6|I **finished** my tasks yesterday.|I **have already finished** all my tasks.|\n|7|The supplier **sent** us the packing list two days ago.|The supplier **has just sent** us the packing list.|\n|8|We **found** mistakes in the invoice during yesterday's check.|We **have found** two mistakes so far.|\n|9|She **was** responsible for this report last year.|She **has been** responsible for this report since January.|\n|10|I **talked** to the production team about this problem this morning.|I **have talked** to the production team about this problem several times this week.|\n\n## Pattern học viên cần nhận ra\n\n> **Present Simple:** What usually happens?  \n> I check invoices every day.\n\n> **Past Simple:** What happened at a finished time?  \n> I checked the invoice **yesterday**.\n\n> **Present Perfect:** What has happened up to now?  \n> I have checked three invoices **so far today**.\n\nĐặc biệt câu 9 rất đáng luyện:\n\n> She **is** responsible. → Present Simple  \n> She **was** responsible. → Past Simple  \n> She **has been** responsible. → Present Perfect\n\nĐây cũng là cách rất tốt để học viên luyện luôn chuỗi **is → was → has been**.","x":13260,"y":5300,"width":740,"height":4300},
		{"id":"d37f362703de9611","type":"text","text":"\n## 8. TENSE 6: TALKING ABOUT THE FUTURE\n\n### WILL\n\nUse for:\n\n**Decision now**\n\nI’ll check it.\n\n**Promise**\n\nI’ll send you an update.\n\n**Prediction**\n\nI think the shipment will arrive tomorrow.\n\n### BE GOING TO\n\nUse for an **existing intention or plan.**\n\nWe’re going to **review the report** tomorrow.\n\n### PRESENT CONTINUOUS FOR ARRANGEMENTS -- 90-95%\n\n**Use when something is already arranged.**\n\nI’m meeting the supplier at 2 p.m.\n\n### Compare\n\nSituation:\n\nYour colleague asks:\n\n“Can you check this file?”\n\nYou decide now:\n\nSure, I’ll check it.\n\nYou already planned it:\n\nI’m going to check it this afternoon.\n\nYou already have a scheduled review:\n\nI’m reviewing it with Finance at 3 p.m.\n\n**","x":12080,"y":17280,"width":650,"height":1100},
		{"id":"d3d7263fb12a8b93","type":"text","text":"**PPJ BUSINESS ENGLISH SPEAKING & LISTENING\n\n***SESSION 8: EXPLAINING A WORK PROBLEM CLEARLY***\n\nLevel: A1–A2  \nWorkplace theme: Problems, causes, impact and next actions  \nSpeaking outcome: Bạn có thể giải thích một vấn đề công việc theo trình tự rõ ràng, không đổ lỗi và đề xuất bước xử lý tiếp theo.\n\nTODAY I CAN\n\n□ I can state t**he main problem clearly**.  \n□ I can explain **what caused the problem.**  \n□ I can describe **how the problem affects the work.**  \n□ I can give the **current status.**  \n□ I can **suggest a clear next action**.  \n□ I can give a short issue update **without blaming anyone.**\n\n1. READING\n\nA Clear Problem Report\n\nMinh works in the Warehouse Department. \nOn Wednesday morning, his team receives a delivery from a supplier. \nAccording to the packing list, the shipment should contain 500 pieces. \nHowever, the warehouse team only counts 480 pieces.\n\nMinh first wants to tell Purchasing team\n\n“The supplier made a mistake. The shipment is wrong.”\n\nHowever, this message does not give enough information. \nIt also sounds like Minh is blaming the supplier before the team checks all the documents.\n\nMinh gives a clearer update:\n\n“We found a **quantity difference** in today’s delivery. \nThe **packing list shows 500 pieces**, but we **received 480 pieces**. \nWe may be missing 20 pieces. \nAs a result, we cannot complete the receiving process yet. \nWe are currently \n- ***checking the cartons*** and \n- ***cross-checking the delivery documents***. \nCould Purchasing Team **contact the supplier** and **confirm the shipped quantity**?”\n\n**Purchasing team** understands the problem and contacts the supplier. \nThe supplier checks the records and says that ***one carton was not loaded onto the truck***. \nThey agree to send the missing carton **the next day.**\n\n> A clear problem report should explain the \n> - **problem,** \n> - **cause,** \n> - **impact,** \n> - **current status** \n> - **and next action.** \n\n**It should focus on *facts and solutions* instead of blame.**\n\nNEW VOCABULARY\n\n|Word or phrase|Meaning in Vietnamese|\n|---|---|\n|issue|vấn đề cần xử lý|\n|quantity difference|chênh lệch số lượng|\n|according to|theo như, căn cứ theo|\n|receive|nhận hàng|\n|missing|bị thiếu|\n|cause|nguyên nhân|\n|impact|ảnh hưởng|\n|current status|trạng thái hiện tại|\n|cross-check|kiểm tra chéo|\n|receiving process|quy trình nhận hàng|\n|take action|thực hiện hành động|\n|blame|đổ lỗi|\n|shipment record|hồ sơ lô hàng|\n|next step|bước tiếp theo|\n\nMULTIPLE CHOICE QUESTIONS\n\n1. How many pieces are shown on the packing list?\n\nA. 420 pieces  \nB. 480 pieces  \n**C. 500 pieces**  \nD. 520 pieces\n\n2. How many pieces does the **warehouse receive**?\n\nA. 450 pieces  \n**B. 480 pieces**  \nC. 500 pieces  \nD. 580 pieces\n\n3. Why does Minh change his first message?\n\nA. It is too polite.  \nB. It is too long.  \n**C. It does not give enough information and sounds blaming.**  \nD. It contains the wrong supplier name.\n\n4. What is the warehouse team doing now?\n\nA. Preparing the payment  \n**B. Checking the cartons and delivery documents**  \nC. Sending the goods back  \nD. Updating the invoice amount\n\n5. What is the **main lesson of the reading**?\n\n**A. A good report should focus on facts and solutions.**  \nB. The supplier should always be blamed first.  \nC. Every problem should be reported in one sentence.  \nD. The warehouse should solve every issue alone.\n\n2. TONE LADDER\n\n**LEVEL 1: BAD, UNCLEAR OR BLAMING**\n\nThe supplier made a mistake.\n\nThe shipment is wrong.\n\nWarehouse did not check the goods properly.\n\nThis problem is not our responsibility.\n\nThese statements may create tension because they:\n\n1. blame a person or department;\n    \n2. do not give clear facts;\n    \n3. do not explain the impact;\n    \n4. do not suggest a solution.\n    \n\n**LEVEL 2: POLITE AND FACTUAL**\n\nThere is a quantity difference in today’s delivery.\n\nThe packing list shows 500 pieces, but we received 480 pieces.\n\nWe are currently checking the cartons and documents.\n\nThese statements describe the facts without attacking anyone.\n\n**LEVEL 3: COLLABORATIVE AND SOLUTION FOCUSED**\n\nWe found a quantity difference in today’s delivery.\n\nAs a result, we cannot complete the receiving process yet.\n\nWe are checking the cartons and cross-checking the documents.\n\nCould Import–Export *contact the supplier and confirm the shipped quantity*?\n\n**CLEAR PROBLEM REPORT FLOW**\n\n**Problem**\n\n**→ Cause**\n\n**→ Impact**\n\n**→ Current status**\n\n**→ Next action**\n\nCOMPLETE MODEL\n\nWe found a quantity difference in today’s delivery. The packing list shows 500 pieces, but Warehouse received 480 pieces. The issue may be related to one missing carton. As a result, we cannot complete the receiving process yet. We are currently checking the cartons and delivery documents. Could Import–Export contact the supplier and confirm the shipped quantity?\n\n3. EXPRESSION BANK\n\nFUNCTION A: **STATE THE PROBLEM**\n\n| Complete expression                                           | Meaning in Vietnamese                                          | Connected speech rhythm                                     | PPJ workplace example                    | Rapid fire trigger        |\n| ------------------------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------- | ------------------------- |\n| *We found a* **quantity difference** in today’s delivery.     | Chúng tôi phát hiện chênh lệch số lượng trong lô hàng hôm nay. | We found / a QUANtity difference / in today’s delivery.     | Packing list ghi 500 nhưng Kho nhận 480. | Có chênh lệch số lượng.   |\n| *There seems to be an* **issue with the shipment documents**. | Có vẻ đang có vấn đề với chứng từ lô hàng.                     | There seems to be / an ISSUE / with the shipment documents. | Packing list và invoice không khớp.      | Chứng từ có vấn đề.       |\n| The information in **the system does not match the report.**  | Thông tin trên hệ thống không khớp với báo cáo.                | The information / in the SYStem / doesn’t match the report. | WFX hiển thị quantity khác file báo cáo. | System không khớp report. |\n\nFUNCTION B: EXPLAIN THE CAUSE\n\n| Complete expression                                                 | Meaning in Vietnamese                                               | Connected speech rhythm                                  | PPJ workplace example                   | Rapid fire trigger     |\n| ------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------- | ---------------------- |\n| ***The issue* may be related to** - **a missing car-ton.**          | Vấn đề có thể liên quan đến một thùng hàng bị thiếu.                | The issue may be reLAted / to a missing carton.          | Kho nhận thiếu một carton.              | Có thể thiếu carton.   |\n| **I*t happened because*** -  the supplier **used an old document.** | Việc này xảy ra vì supplier sử dụng chứng từ cũ.                    | It happened beCAUSE / the supplier used an old document. | Supplier gửi packing list phiên bản cũ. | Supplier dùng file cũ. |\n| We **cannot confirm the cause** *until we check the records.*       | Chúng tôi chưa thể xác nhận nguyên nhân cho đến khi kiểm tra hồ sơ. | We can’t confirm the CAUSE / until we check the records. | Chưa đủ dữ liệu để kết luận.            | Chưa rõ nguyên nhân.   |\n\n  \n\nFUNCTION C: EXPLAIN THE IMPACT AND STATUS\n\n| Complete expression                                                      | Meaning in Vietnamese                                        | Connected speech rhythm                                      | PPJ workplace example              | Rapid fire trigger           |\n| ------------------------------------------------------------------------ | ------------------------------------------------------------ | ------------------------------------------------------------ | ---------------------------------- | ---------------------------- |\n| **As a result**, **we cannot complete** + (n) the receiving process yet. | Vì vậy, chúng tôi chưa thể hoàn tất quy trình nhận hàng.     | As a reSULT / we can’t complete / the receiving process yet. | Kho chưa thể hoàn tất receiving.   | Chưa thể hoàn tất receiving. |\n| The issue may **delay the shipment update.**                             | Vấn đề có thể làm chậm việc cập nhật lô hàng.                | The issue may deLAY / the shipment update.                   | XNK chưa thể báo status chính xác. | Có thể chậm shipment update. |\n| **We are currently checking** the cartons and documents.                 | Hiện tại chúng tôi đang kiểm tra các thùng hàng và chứng từ. | We’re currently CHECKing / the cartons and documents.        | Kho đang kiểm đếm lại.             | Đang kiểm tra.               |\n\nFUNCTION D: PROPOSE THE NEXT ACTION\n\n| Complete expression                                              | Meaning in Vietnamese                                   | Connected speech rhythm                                        | PPJ workplace example                  | Rapid fire trigger         |\n| ---------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------- | -------------------------- |\n| **Could you** *contact the supplier and confirm the quantity?*   | Bạn có thể liên hệ supplier và xác nhận số lượng không? | Could you CONtact the supplier / and confirm the quantity?     | XNK cần hỏi supplier số lượng đã gửi.  | Nhờ xác nhận với supplier. |\n| Let’s **cross-check the documents** before we update the system. | Hãy kiểm tra chéo chứng từ trước khi cập nhật hệ thống. | Let’s cross-check the DOCuments / before we update the system. | PO, invoice và packing list khác nhau. | Kiểm tra trước khi update. |\n| **I’ll send another update** when we have more information.      | Tôi sẽ gửi cập nhật khác khi có thêm thông tin.         | I’ll send another UPdate / when we have more information.      | Chưa có kết quả cuối cùng.             | Sẽ cập nhật thêm.          |\n\n4. RAPID FIRE PRACTICE\n\nBạn nghe trigger tiếng Việt và trả lời trong 5 giây.\n\n|Trigger|Complete response|\n|---|---|\n|Có chênh lệch số lượng|We found a quantity difference in today’s delivery.|\n|Chứng từ có vấn đề|There seems to be an issue with the shipment documents.|\n|System không khớp report|The information in the system does not match the report.|\n|Có thể thiếu carton|The issue may be related to a missing carton.|\n|Supplier dùng file cũ|It happened because the supplier used an old document.|\n|Chưa rõ nguyên nhân|We cannot confirm the cause until we check the records.|\n|Chưa thể hoàn tất receiving|As a result, we cannot complete the receiving process yet.|\n|Có thể chậm shipment update|The issue may delay the shipment update.|\n|Đang kiểm tra|We are currently checking the cartons and documents.|\n|Nhờ xác nhận với supplier|Could you contact the supplier and confirm the quantity?|\n|Kiểm tra trước khi update|Let’s cross-check the documents before we update the system.|\n|Sẽ cập nhật thêm|I’ll send another update when we have more information.|\n\n  \n\n5. THREE SPEAKING PATTERNS\n\nPATTERN 1: **STATE THE PROBLEM WITH FACTS**\n\nFramework\n\nWe found a problem with + document, quantity or system.\n\n\nThe document shows + expected information.\n\n\nHowever, the actual information is + actual information.\n\nSample answer\n\nWe found a problem with the received quantity. The packing list shows 500 pieces. However, the actual received quantity is 480 pieces.\n\nBUILD YOUR ANSWER\n\n|Information|Your notes|\n|---|---|\n|Problem||\n|Expected information||\n|Actual information||\n\nSay your answer to your partner.\n\nYour partner asks:\n\nWhen did you find the problem?\n\nAdd the time to your explanation.\n\nPATTERN 2: EXPLAIN THE CAUSE AND IMPACT\n\nFramework\n\nThe issue may be related to + possible cause.\n\n  \n\nAs a result, + business impact.\n\n  \n\nWe cannot + affected task yet.\n\nSample answer\n\nThe issue may be related to one missing carton. As a result, the received quantity does not match the packing list. We cannot complete the receiving process yet.\n\nBUILD YOUR ANSWER\n\n|Information|Your notes|\n|---|---|\n|Possible cause||\n|Business impact||\n|Affected task||\n\nSay your answer twice.\n\nRound 1: Use your notes.\n\nRound 2: Speak without reading the complete framework.\n\nPATTERN 3: GIVE A COMPLETE ISSUE UPDATE\n\nFramework\n\nThe main issue is + problem.\n\n  \n\nIt happened because or may be related to + cause.\n\n  \n\nAs a result, + impact.\n\n  \n\nWe are currently + status.\n\n  \n\nThe next step is to + action.\n\n  \n\nI’ll update you again by + time.\n\nSample answer\n\nThe main issue is a quantity difference in today’s delivery. It may be related to one missing carton. As a result, Warehouse cannot complete the receiving process. We are currently checking the cartons and delivery documents. The next step is to contact the supplier and confirm the shipped quantity. I’ll update you again by two p.m.\n\nBUILD YOUR ANSWER\n\n|Part|Your notes|\n|---|---|\n|Main issue||\n|Cause||\n|Impact||\n|Current status||\n|Next action||\n|Next update time||\n\nGive a 60 to 90 second issue update to your partner.\n\n6. TRANSFORM THE SENTENCES\n\nChange each unclear, emotional or blaming statement into a factual and collaborative explanation.\n\n| Unclear or blaming statement            | Your professional version                                                                                                                                      |\n| --------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| The supplier made a mistake.            | the supplier ***might have made*** a mistake in sending the documents                                                                                          |\n| The shipment is wrong.                  | Hi there, the shipment has an issue in quantity / total value / the VAT / ... can you contact the supplier and ask them to recheck all the shipping documents? |\n| Warehouse lost the goods.               |                                                                                                                                                                |\n| Accounting entered the wrong number.    |                                                                                                                                                                |\n| IT has not fixed the system.            |                                                                                                                                                                |\n| The documents are a mess.               |                                                                                                                                                                |\n| We cannot do anything.                  |                                                                                                                                                                |\n| This problem is not our responsibility. |                                                                                                                                                                |\n| Someone used the wrong file.            |                                                                                                                                                                |\n| Everything is delayed now.              |                                                                                                                                                                |\n\nUSE THIS CHECKLIST\n\nA strong transformation should include at least three elements.\n\n□ A clear fact  \n□ A possible cause  \n□ A business impact  \n□ A current status  \n□ A next action  \n□ No personal blame\n\nMODEL TRANSFORMATION\n\nBlaming\n\nThe supplier sent the wrong document.\n\nProfessional\n\nThe document we received does not match the latest PO. The issue may be related to an older file version. We are checking the document history and will ask the supplier to send the updated version.\n\n7. ROLEPLAY: QUANTITY DIFFERENCE IN A DELIVERY\n\nSITUATION\n\nWarehouse receives a delivery for PO 6725.\n\n|Information|Details|\n|---|---|\n|Quantity on the packing list|1,000 pieces|\n|Quantity received|960 pieces|\n|Difference|40 pieces|\n|Possible cause|One carton may be missing|\n|Current status|Warehouse is recounting the goods|\n|Business impact|GRN cannot be completed|\n|Required action|XNK must contact the supplier|\n|Next internal update|Before 3:00 p.m.|\n\n  \n  \n\nROLE A: WAREHOUSE\n\nYou found the quantity difference.\n\nExplain:\n\n1. what the packing list shows;\n    \n2. what Warehouse received;\n    \n3. the possible cause;\n    \n4. how the issue affects the receiving process;\n    \n5. what Warehouse is doing now;\n    \n6. what you need from XNK.\n    \n\nUse at least five expressions.\n\nWe found a quantity difference in today’s delivery.\n\nThe packing list shows 1,000 pieces, but we received 960 pieces.\n\nThe issue may be related to one missing carton.\n\nAs a result, we cannot complete the GRN yet.\n\nWe are currently recounting the goods.\n\nCould you contact the supplier and confirm the shipped quantity?\n\nROLE B: IMPORT EXPORT\n\nYou need clear information before contacting the supplier.\n\nAsk:\n\n1. when the goods arrived;\n    \n2. whether all cartons were checked;\n    \n3. whether the packing list is the latest version;\n    \n4. what information the supplier needs to confirm;\n    \n5. when Warehouse can give the recount result.\n    \n\nUse at least five expressions.\n\nWhen did the delivery arrive?\n\nHave you checked all the cartons?\n\nIs this the latest version of the packing list?\n\nWhat quantity should I confirm with the supplier?\n\nWhen can you complete the recount?\n\nI’ll contact the supplier and send you an update before three p.m.  \n\nCONVERSATION FLOW\n\nState the problem\n\n→ Give the numbers\n\n→ Ask clarification questions\n\n→ Explain the possible cause\n\n→ Explain the impact\n\n→ Give the current status\n\n→ Agree on the next action\n\n→ Confirm the update time\n\nFINAL ISSUE SUMMARY\n\nComplete the summary together.\n\n|Item|Agreed information|\n|---|---|\n|The main problem is||\n|The expected quantity is||\n|The received quantity is||\n|The possible cause is||\n|Warehouse is currently||\n|Import–Export will||\n|The next update will be before||\n\nSUCCESS CHECK\n\n□ I explained the problem with clear facts.  \n□ I gave the expected and actual numbers.  \n□ I explained a possible cause without blaming anyone.  \n□ I described the business impact.  \n□ I gave the current status.  \n□ I proposed or accepted a next action.  \n□ We agreed on the next update time.\n\n8. SESSION TAKEAWAY\n\nA clear work problem explanation should not be:\n\nThe shipment is wrong, and the supplier made a mistake.\n\nUse this structure:\n\nProblem\n\n+\n\nCause\n\n+\n\nImpact\n\n+\n\nCurrent status\n\n+\n\nNext action\n\nFIVE SENTENCES TO REMEMBER\n\nWe found a quantity difference in today’s delivery.\n\nThe issue may be related to a missing carton.\n\nAs a result, we cannot complete the receiving process yet.\n\nWe are currently checking the cartons and documents.\n\nCould you contact the supplier and confirm the quantity?\n\nFINAL MESSAGE\n\nExplain the facts, show the impact and move the conversation toward a solution.\n\n  \n**","x":13200,"y":9760,"width":960,"height":12480},
		{"id":"7883e47d7307a136","type":"text","text":"homework 1: hoan thanh viet lai cau - worksheet grammar - tenses","x":8800,"y":11220,"width":250,"height":86},
		{"id":"0dca002e75cae8d6","type":"text","text":"EXPLAIN:\n- S + NEED + OBJECT + TO DO SOMETHING \n- S + TO-BE(AM/IS/ARE - WAS/WERE - BEEN) + WAITING + FOR + OBJECT + TO DO SMTHING\n","x":8860,"y":15000,"width":220,"height":260},
		{"id":"f12a8cacaebf7fd5","type":"text","text":"CHECK CURRENT STATS:\n- DO YOU HAVE ANY UPDATE ON + ...\n- COULD YOU UPDATE ME ON + ....","x":8870,"y":14600,"width":210,"height":220},
		{"id":"a44c7311d720dd67","type":"text","text":"Follow up:\n- Just checking in on\n- I'm following up on ","x":8870,"y":14300,"width":210,"height":125},
		{"id":"25e255c525c1c0d4","type":"text","text":"TIME- ARRANGING + CLOSE:\n\n- COULD YOU PLEASE UPDATE + SOMEBODY + BY + TIME ?\n- WHEN CAN WE / I EXPECT + OBJECT?","x":8860,"y":15300,"width":220,"height":240},
		{"id":"a3bcc42b181cde8b","type":"text","text":"homework 2: record + submit your reading of passage ->>","x":8700,"y":11400,"width":250,"height":89},
		{"id":"fc62c48a4ae414a7","type":"text","text":"","x":15600,"y":19080,"width":1160,"height":100},
		{"id":"515adcc81083fca6","type":"text","text":"PPJ BUSINESS ENGLISH SPEAKING & LISTENING\n\n**SESSION 9: MISTAKES, RESPONSIBILITY & SOLUTIONS**\n\nLevel: A1–A2  \nWorkplace theme: Mistakes, responsibility and problem solving  \nSpeaking outcome: Bạn có thể nói về sai sót một cách bình tĩnh, nhận trách nhiệm phù hợp và đề xuất cách xử lý mà không đổ lỗi cho người khác.\n\nTODAY I CAN\n\n□ I can explain what went wrong.  \n□ I can acknowledge a mistake professionally.  \n□ I can avoid blaming another person or department.  \n□ I can explain what I am checking now.  \n□ I can suggest a clear solution.  \n□ I can confirm the next action and update time.\n\n1. READING\n\n**From a Mistake to a Solution**\n\nLan works in the Finance and Accounting Department. \nOn Thursday morning, she checks a supplier invoice in the system. \nThe invoice shows **580 pieces, but the purchase order shows 850 pieces.**\n\nLan first thinks that Warehouse entered the wrong quantity. She wants to write:\n\n“Warehouse entered the wrong number. Please fix it.”\n\nHowever, Lan has not checked all the information yet. \nHer message may sound blaming and could **create tension** between the departments. \n\nLan contacts Warehouse and says:\n\n“There seems to be a quantity difference between the invoice and the PO. \nI may have used an older document, so I’m checking the file history now. \nCould you help me confirm the actual received quantity?”\n\nWarehouse checks the receiving record. \nThe actual quantity is 580 pieces. \n**The supplier used an old PO number on the invoice.**\n\nLan then gives an update:\n\n“We found the cause of the difference. \nThe supplier used an old PO reference.\nI’ll contact the supplier and ask for a corrected invoice. \nWe can continue the payment process after we receive the new document.”\n\nLan **does not hide the problem or blame another department**. \nShe ***checks the facts, explains the cause and moves the conversation toward a solution.***\n\nNEW VOCABULARY\n\n|Word or phrase|Meaning in Vietnamese|\n|---|---|\n|mistake|sai sót|\n|responsibility|trách nhiệm|\n|take responsibility|nhận trách nhiệm|\n|blame|đổ lỗi|\n|incorrect|không chính xác|\n|quantity difference|chênh lệch số lượng|\n|file history|lịch sử file|\n|receiving record|hồ sơ nhận hàng|\n|old reference|mã tham chiếu cũ|\n|corrected invoice|hóa đơn đã chỉnh sửa|\n|solve the issue|giải quyết vấn đề|\n|prevent|ngăn ngừa|\n|next action|hành động tiếp theo|\n|happen again|xảy ra lần nữa|\n\nMULTIPLE CHOICE QUESTIONS\n\n1. What problem does Lan find?\n\nA. The supplier has not sent the invoice.  \n**B. The invoice and PO show different quantities.**  \nC. Warehouse has lost the goods.  \nD. The system is not working.\n\n2. Why does Lan avoid blaming Warehouse?\n\nA. She does not know anyone in Warehouse.  \n**B. She has not checked all the facts yet.**  \nC. The invoice is not important.  \nD. Warehouse cannot change the information.\n\n3. What is the actual received quantity?\n\nA. 500 pieces  \n**B. 580 pieces**  \nC. 800 pieces  \nD. 850 pieces\n\n4. What caused the problem?\n\nA. Finance entered the wrong payment amount.  \nB. Warehouse used the wrong receiving record.  \n**C. The supplier used an old PO reference.**  \nD. The shipment arrived late.\n\n5. What does Lan do next?\n\nA. She cancels the payment.  \nB. She asks Warehouse to change the quantity.  \n**C. She asks the supplier for a corrected invoice.**  \nD. She creates a new purchase order.\n\n2. TONE LADDER\n\n**LEVEL 1: BLAMING OR UNPROFESSIONAL**\n\n- You entered the wrong number.\n\n- This is Warehouse’s mistake.\n\n- The supplier always sends incorrect documents.\n\n- This problem is not my responsibility.\n\n> These statements may **create tension** because they **focus on the person instead of the problem**.\n\n**LEVEL 2: POLITE AND FACTUAL**\n\n**There seems to be** a quantity difference.\n\nThe invoice does not match the PO.\n\nWe need to check which document is correct.\n\n- These statements ( loi khang dinh / loi noi / loi nhan dinh / loi phat bieu / loi tuyen bo) describe the facts without blaming anyone.\n\n**LEVEL 3: COLLABORATIVE AND SOLUTION FOCUSED**\n\n> There seems to be a quantity difference between the invoice and the PO.\n> \n> I may have used an older document, so I’m checking the file history now.\n> \n> Could you help me confirm the actual received quantity?\n> \n> After we confirm the cause, we can ask the supplier to correct the invoice.\n\nSOLUTION FOCUSED FLOW\n\n- [ ] **Acknowledge the issue**\n\n- [ ] **→ Check the facts**\n\n- [ ] **→ Explain the cause**\n\n- [ ] **→ Take responsibility**\n\n- [ ] **→ Suggest a solution**\n\n- [ ] **→ Prevent the problem from happening again**\n\nCOMPLETE MODEL\n\nThere seems to be a quantity difference between the invoice and the PO. I may have used an older document, so I’m checking the file history now. Could you help me confirm the actual received quantity? After that, I’ll contact the supplier and ask for a corrected invoice. I’ll update you again before three p.m.\n\n3. EXPRESSION BANK\n\nFUNCTION A: ACKNOWLEDGE THE MISTAKE\n\n|Complete expression|Meaning in Vietnamese|Connected speech rhythm|PPJ workplace example|Rapid fire trigger|\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n|It looks like there is a mistake in the report.|Có vẻ như có một sai sót trong báo cáo.|It looks like / there’s-a misTAKE / in-the-report.|Số lượng trong report không khớp WFX.|Báo cáo có sai sót.|\n|I may have missed an important detail.|Có thể tôi đã bỏ sót một chi tiết quan trọng.|I-may-have MISSED / an-important-detail.|Bạn chưa kiểm tra phiên bản mới nhất của file.|Có thể tôi bỏ sót.|\n|I’m sorry about the confusion.|Tôi xin lỗi về sự nhầm lẫn này.|I’m SORry / about-the-confusion.|Thông tin bạn gửi khiến phòng ban khác hiểu nhầm.|Xin lỗi vì nhầm lẫn.|\n\nFUNCTION B: CHECK WHAT HAPPENED\n\n| Complete expression                                           | Meaning in Vietnamese                                | Connected speech rhythm                                 | PPJ workplace example             | Rapid fire trigger          |\n| ------------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------- | --------------------------- |\n| **Let’s check what happened** before **we make any changes.** | Hãy kiểm tra chuyện gì đã xảy ra trước khi thay đổi. | Let’s CHECK what-happened / before-we-make-any-changes. | Chưa biết PO hay invoice đúng.    | Kiểm tra trước khi sửa.     |\n| **We need to confirm** ***which document is correct.***       | Chúng ta cần xác nhận chứng từ nào là đúng.          | We-need-to-conFIRM / which-document-is-correct.         | Hai file có số lượng khác nhau.   | Xác nhận file đúng.         |\n| I’m checking the **file history** now.                        | Hiện tại tôi đang kiểm tra lịch sử file.             | I’m CHECKing / the-file-history-now.                    | Có thể team đã dùng phiên bản cũ. | Đang kiểm tra lịch sử file. |\n\nFUNCTION C: TAKE RESPONSIBILITY PROFESSIONALLY\n\n| Complete expression                                     | Meaning in Vietnamese                             | Connected speech rhythm                                | PPJ workplace example                | Rapid fire trigger        |\n| ------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------ | ------------------------------------ | ------------------------- |\n| **I should have checked** the latest version first.     | Lẽ ra tôi nên kiểm tra phiên bản mới nhất trước.  | I-should-have CHECKED / the-latest-version-first.      | Bạn dùng file cũ để cập nhật report. | Lẽ ra nên kiểm tra trước. |\n| **I’ll take responsibility for** correcting the report. | Tôi sẽ chịu trách nhiệm chỉnh sửa báo cáo.        | I’ll-take-responsiBILity / for-correcting-the-report.  | Bạn cần cập nhật lại số liệu.        | Tôi sẽ sửa báo cáo.       |\n| I’ll make sure the information is updated correctly.    | Tôi sẽ đảm bảo thông tin được cập nhật chính xác. | I’ll-make SURE / the-information-is-updated-correctly. | Bạn sẽ sửa dữ liệu trên system.      | Đảm bảo update đúng.      |\n\n  \n\nFUNCTION D: PROPOSE A SOLUTION\n\n| Complete expression                                                        | Meaning in Vietnamese                                  | Connected speech rhythm                                                        | PPJ workplace example                  | Rapid fire trigger |\n| -------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------ | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------ | -------------------------------------- | ------------------ |\n| Here’s how we can **fix the issue.**                                       | Đây là cách chúng ta có thể xử lý vấn đề.              | Here’s-how-we-can FIX / the-issue.                                             | Bạn bắt đầu trình bày phương án xử lý. | Đây là cách xử lý. |\n| I’ll contact the supplier and request a corrected invoice.                 | Tôi sẽ liên hệ supplier và yêu cầu hóa đơn điều chỉnh. | I’ll-CONtact-the-supplier / and-request-a-corrected-invoice.                   | Invoice sử dụng PO reference cũ.       | Xin invoice mới.   |\n| We should add one more checking step to prevent this from happening again. | Chúng ta nên thêm một bước kiểm tra để tránh lặp lại.  | We-should-add / one-more-CHECKing-step / to-prevent-this-from-happening-again. | Team muốn ngăn lỗi file version.       | Ngăn lỗi lặp lại.  |\n\n4. RAPID FIRE PRACTICE\n\nBạn nghe trigger tiếng Việt và trả lời trong 5 giây.\n\n|Trigger|Complete response|\n|---|---|\n|Báo cáo có sai sót|It looks like there is a mistake in the report.|\n|Có thể tôi bỏ sót|I may have missed an important detail.|\n|Xin lỗi vì nhầm lẫn|I’m sorry about the confusion.|\n|Kiểm tra trước khi sửa|Let’s check what happened before we make any changes.|\n|Xác nhận file đúng|We need to confirm which document is correct.|\n|Đang kiểm tra lịch sử file|I’m checking the file history now.|\n|Lẽ ra nên kiểm tra trước|I should have checked the latest version first.|\n|Tôi sẽ sửa báo cáo|I’ll take responsibility for correcting the report.|\n|Đảm bảo update đúng|I’ll make sure the information is updated correctly.|\n|Đây là cách xử lý|Here’s how we can fix the issue.|\n|Xin invoice mới|I’ll contact the supplier and request a corrected invoice.|\n|Ngăn lỗi lặp lại|We should add one more checking step to prevent this from happening again.|\n\n5. THREE SPEAKING PATTERNS\n\nPATTERN 1: ACKNOWLEDGE AND CHECK\n\nFramework\n\nIt looks like there is a mistake in + document or system.\n\n  \n\nI may have + possible mistake.\n\n  \n\nI’m checking + information now.\n\nSample answer\n\nIt looks like there is a mistake in the payment report. I may have used an older invoice. I’m checking the document history now.\n\nBUILD YOUR ANSWER\n\n|Information|Your notes|\n|---|---|\n|Document or system||\n|Possible mistake||\n|Information you are checking||\n\nSay your answer to your partner.\n\nYour partner asks:\n\nWhen can you confirm the correct information?\n\nAdd a clear update time.\n\nPATTERN 2: TAKE RESPONSIBILITY AND FIX THE ISSUE\n\nFramework\n\nI should have + correct action.\n\n  \n\nI’ll take responsibility for + correction.\n\n  \n\nI’ll complete the correction by + time.\n\nSample answer\n\nI should have checked the latest file first. I’ll take responsibility for correcting the report. I’ll complete the correction by two p.m.\n\nBUILD YOUR ANSWER\n\n|Information|Your notes|\n|---|---|\n|What you should have done||\n|What you will correct||\n|Completion time||\n\nSay your complete answer twice.\n\nRound 1: Use your notes.\n\nRound 2: Speak without reading the framework.\n\nPATTERN 3: COMPLETE SOLUTION UPDATE\n\nFramework\n\nThe issue is + problem.\n\n  \n\nIt happened because + cause.\n\n  \n\nI may have or our team may have + responsibility.\n\n  \n\nHere’s how we can fix it.\n\n  \n\nFirst, we will + action one.\n\n  \n\nThen, we will + action two.\n\n  \n\nTo prevent this from happening again, we will + prevention.\n\nSample answer\n\nThe issue is an incorrect quantity in the payment report. It happened because the supplier used an old PO reference. I may have missed the difference during the first check. Here’s how we can fix it. First, I’ll ask the supplier for a corrected invoice. Then, I’ll update the report. To prevent this from happening again, we’ll check the PO reference before processing each invoice.\n\nBUILD YOUR ANSWER\n\n|Part|Your notes|\n|---|---|\n|Problem||\n|Cause||\n|Responsibility||\n|Action one||\n|Action two||\n|Prevention||\n\nGive a 60 to 90 second solution update to your partner.\n\n6. TRANSFORM THE SENTENCES\n\nChange each blaming or unprofessional sentence into a factual, responsible and solution focused statement.\n\n|Blaming or unprofessional sentence|Your professional version|\n|---|---|\n|You entered the wrong quantity.||\n|This is Warehouse’s mistake.||\n|Accounting did not check the invoice.||\n|The supplier always sends bad documents.||\n|IT caused the whole problem.||\n|This is not my responsibility.||\n|Someone used the wrong file again.||\n|You need to fix this now.||\n|We cannot continue because of your mistake.||\n|This problem keeps happening because nobody checks anything.||\n\nUSE THIS CHECKLIST\n\nA strong transformation should include at least three elements.\n\n□ A clear fact  \n□ No personal blame  \n□ A possible cause  \n□ Appropriate responsibility  \n□ A solution  \n□ A prevention step\n\nMODEL TRANSFORMATION\n\nBlaming\n\nWarehouse entered the wrong number.\n\nProfessional\n\nThe quantity in the system does not match the receiving record. We need to confirm which number is correct. After that, I’ll update the report and inform the related teams.\n\n7. ROLEPLAY: WRONG QUANTITY ENTERED IN THE SYSTEM\n\nSITUATION\n\nA quantity was entered incorrectly in the system for PO 7730.\n\n|Information|Details|\n|---|---|\n|Actual received quantity|720 pieces|\n|Quantity in the system|270 pieces|\n|Person who entered the data|Warehouse staff member|\n|Possible cause|The digits were entered in the wrong order|\n|Business impact|Finance cannot complete the invoice check|\n|Current action|Warehouse is checking the receiving record|\n|Required correction|Update the system quantity to 720|\n|Next update|Before 2:30 p.m.|\n\n  \n  \n\nROLE A: FINANCE AND ACCOUNTING\n\nYou found the difference while checking the supplier invoice.\n\nYou need to:\n\n1. explain the difference without blaming Warehouse;\n    \n2. ask Warehouse to confirm the actual quantity;\n    \n3. explain the impact on invoice checking;\n    \n4. ask when the system can be corrected;\n    \n5. confirm the next action.\n    \n\nUse at least five sentences.\n\nThere seems to be a quantity difference in the system.\n\nThe invoice shows 720 pieces, but the system shows 270 pieces.\n\nCould you help me confirm the actual received quantity?\n\nWe cannot complete the invoice check until the quantity is confirmed.\n\nWhen can the system information be corrected?\n\nPlease send me an update when the correction is complete.\n\nROLE B: WAREHOUSE\n\nYou entered the quantity into the system.\n\nYou discover that the digits were entered in the wrong order.\n\nYou need to:\n\n1. acknowledge the mistake;\n    \n2. explain what happened;\n    \n3. take responsibility for correcting it;\n    \n4. give a completion time;\n    \n5. suggest a prevention step.\n    \n\nUse at least five sentences.\n\nIt looks like I entered the digits in the wrong order.\n\nI’m sorry about the confusion.\n\nI should have checked the quantity before saving the record.\n\nI’ll take responsibility for correcting the system information.\n\nI can complete the correction before two thirty.\n\nWe should add a second check before saving quantity data.  \n\nCONVERSATION FLOW\n\nState the difference\n\n→ Ask for confirmation\n\n→ Check what happened\n\n→ Acknowledge the mistake\n\n→ Explain the business impact\n\n→ Agree on the correction\n\n→ Confirm the update time\n\n→ Suggest a prevention step\n\nFINAL AGREEMENT\n\nComplete the summary together.\n\n|Item|Agreed information|\n|---|---|\n|The actual quantity is||\n|The system quantity is||\n|The cause of the mistake is||\n|Warehouse will||\n|Finance will continue after||\n|The correction will be completed by||\n|To prevent the problem, the team will||\n\nSUCCESS CHECK\n\n□ I described the facts without blaming anyone.  \n□ I acknowledged the mistake professionally.  \n□ I explained what caused the mistake.  \n□ I accepted an appropriate level of responsibility.  \n□ I proposed a clear correction.  \n□ We agreed on a completion time.  \n□ We suggested a prevention step.\n\n8. SESSION TAKEAWAY\n\nProfessional responsibility does not mean:\n\nEverything is my fault.\n\nIt means:\n\nAcknowledge the issue\n\n+\n\nCheck the facts\n\n+\n\nAccept your part\n\n+\n\nCorrect the problem\n\n+\n\nPrevent it from happening again\n\nFIVE SENTENCES TO REMEMBER\n\nIt looks like there is a mistake in the report.\n\nI may have missed an important detail.\n\nLet’s check what happened before we make any changes.\n\nI’ll take responsibility for correcting the information.\n\nWe should add one more checking step to prevent this from happening again.\n\nFINAL MESSAGE\n\nFocus less on who made the mistake and more on how the team can correct it.\n\nNội dung được phát triển theo mục tiêu Session 9 trong course master plan: chuyển từ blame language sang solution language và xử lý tình huống sai số lượng trên hệ thống.\n\n  \n**","x":14580,"y":9900,"width":920,"height":20093},
		{"id":"9e0293cc0632c760","type":"text","text":"2005\n- two thousands and five ","x":14119,"y":6960,"width":250,"height":73},
		{"id":"692f7b3ae65b9cf9","type":"text","text":"two thousand twenty four\ntwenty twenty-fourth \n","x":14119,"y":6680,"width":250,"height":120},
		{"id":"d806b7d2b63d5268","type":"text","text":"send + somebody + something:\ngui toi ai do cai gi","x":13030,"y":7540,"width":250,"height":91},
		{"id":"e21994e729c1d0d2","type":"text","text":"> am/is/are \n> -> was/were \n> -> been","x":13010,"y":8067,"width":250,"height":93},
		{"id":"7f8e6be71b44e38a","type":"text","text":"cost\ncause","x":14257,"y":10801,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"c4812c004708eb01","type":"text","text":"- IT LOOKS LIKE THERE IS A MISTAKE IN + .... (NOUN)\n- I MAY HAVE MISSED + .....\n- I'M SORRY ABOUT THE CONFUSION ","x":14380,"y":13580,"width":200,"height":260},
		{"id":"0a1a20d662768bf2","type":"text","text":"LE RA :: I SHOULD HAVE + V3/ED\n- I SHOULD HAVE CHECKED THE NEW INVOICE \n- I SHOULD HAVE CHECKED THE CORRECT FABRIC NAME \n- I SHOULD HAVE CHECKED THE QUANTITIES FIRST","x":14400,"y":14220,"width":180,"height":440},
		{"id":"b2d330fa01e916c2","type":"text","text":"I WILL TAKE RESONPSIBILITIES FOR + SOMETHING (NOUN / V_ING)","x":14282,"y":14660,"width":298,"height":54,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"55dd134062050fe6","type":"text","text":"I'LL MAKE SURE TO + DO SOMETHING\nI'LL MAKE SURE + CLAUSE ( S+V+O)","x":14282,"y":14714,"width":298,"height":126,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"cd218705d9fab89e","type":"text","text":"HERE IS + (NOUN/CLAUSE) HOW WE CAN + DO SOMETHING ","x":14282,"y":14840,"width":298,"height":60,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"c5fbfa42757981bf","type":"text","text":"HOW - BANG CACH LA -\nWHAT - VOI CAI MA \nWHERE - O NOI MA \nWHEN - KHI MA\nWHY - TAI VI LA\n","x":14240,"y":15000,"width":250,"height":144,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"fdb5c13dc48219ce","type":"text","text":"HERE'S WHY SHE FIX THE ISSUE","x":14240,"y":15160,"width":250,"height":60,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"52907ab6733d5f09","type":"text","text":"HERE'S WHERE SHE FIX THE PROBLEM\n\nHERE'S THE THING SHE USED TO FIX THE PROBLEM\n","x":14240,"y":15220,"width":250,"height":180,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"8267cdfdbccc568d","type":"text","text":"> talk to somebody about something \n> / talk -> talked -> talked\n> = tell somebody something \n> / tell -> told -> told\n> = share with somebody about something \n> / share-> shared -> shared","x":12920,"y":8240,"width":340,"height":200},
		{"id":"4b6aecb05ccd3a20","type":"text","text":"meet + somebody + (to do something)","x":12920,"y":6991,"width":340,"height":84},
		{"id":"cd619fdfd753d669","type":"text","text":"HOMEWORK 1 - FINISH THE EXERCISE ABOUT P.S + PP IN THE WORKSHEET","x":12991,"y":6020,"width":269,"height":117},
		{"id":"28b589a83181d639","type":"text","text":"homework 1: read aloud and record back ","x":13060,"y":10240,"width":140,"height":140},
		{"id":"9a7caba824ce51af","type":"text","text":"385\n- three eighty-five ","x":12983,"y":10387,"width":217,"height":93},
		{"id":"a242b63e6d44443a","type":"text","text":"2550 - twenty five - fifty  ","x":12983,"y":10480,"width":217,"height":60},
		{"id":"cf334694382e5a04","type":"text","text":"process - present \n- noun: ' am 1'\n- verb: \"am 2\"\n","x":12983,"y":10540,"width":217,"height":200},
		{"id":"5783f6d34b543abd","type":"text","text":"/id/ - t, d\n/t/ - \n/d/ - ","x":13080,"y":13360,"width":111,"height":120},
		{"id":"83b09928062b83e5","type":"text","text":"HOW + VERB + S + OBJECT?\nHOW CAN SHE FIX THE PROBLEM?","x":14150,"y":14360,"width":250,"height":80,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"e68eb0ca09b7241e","type":"text","text":"HOW + S + VERB + OBJECT\nTHAT IS **HOW SHE FIX THE PROBLEM**","x":14150,"y":14440,"width":250,"height":95,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"aaa0feca679155c4","type":"text","text":"homework2: transform 3 sentences of your choosing -> collaborative","x":12950,"y":17840,"width":250,"height":120},
		{"id":"8c1a3d4d1146cec0","type":"text","text":"recheck","x":13080,"y":14920,"width":120,"height":60},
		{"id":"767adda501b65824","type":"text","text":"# IELTS Speaking Part 1 Style\n\n## Daily Chit-Chat + PPJ Workplace Small Talk\n\nBộ này dùng **format câu hỏi kiểu IELTS Speaking Part 1**, nhưng nội dung được chuyển thành **daily English + PPJ workplace English** để học viên vừa luyện IELTS-style response, vừa tạo phản xạ small talk thực tế.\n\nCách này phù hợp với hướng của _Compelling Conversations_: câu hỏi chỉ là **conversation starters**, không phải script; mục tiêu là trả lời rồi phát triển hội thoại bằng follow-up questions. Chapter 2 tập trung vào “Going Beyond Hello”, còn Chapter 6 khai thác routine, schedule, commute và daily habits, rất phù hợp với dạng luyện này.\n\n# 1. MASTER ANSWER PATTERN\n\nKhông trả lời Part 1 bằng một câu cụt.\n\n### Basic pattern\n\n**Answer → Detail → Reason / Example**\n\n> **Q:** Do you usually drink coffee in the morning?  \n> **A:** Yeah, I do. / no i dont \n> I normally **have a coffee** before I start work. **/detail** - frequency :: muc do thuong xuyen\n> It **helps me wake up** **and focus**. **/ reason** \n> LIKE TODAY, I JUST DRANK A SMALL CUP OF COFFEE. **/EXAMPLE**\n\n### PPJ version\n\n> **Q:** Do you usually have a **busy morning** at work?  \n> **A:** \n> **answer/** Yeah, most mornings are **quite busy**. \n> **detail/** I normally **check my emails and pending tasks** first. \n> **reason/** **That /*helps me decide***/ what I need to prioritize.\n\n### Target\n\n**2–4 sentences / 15–25 seconds**\n\nKhông cần nói thành mini presentation.\n\n# 2. TYPE 1 — DO YOU…?\n\n## Habit / General Preference\n\n### Answer pattern\n\n> Yes, I do.  /ANSWER\n> I usually…  /DETAIL\n> \n> Not really. \n> I tend to… instead.\n\n### Daily small talk\n\n1. Do you usually have breakfast before work?\n\t1. answer : yes i do, every morning/ \n\t2. detail: **i usually have**---sticky rice or banh mi, **and sometimes**---- pho or noodles **if i have time** \n\t3. reason /example: like today i just ate banh mi \n\t4. ---> ask back: **so what about you?** / **so how about you? / how is it different from you?**\n\t5. ---> invitation: **would you like to have breakfast / HAVE.. with me sometimes?** / \n    \n2. Do you drink coffee or tea in the morning?\n\t1. answer: no. i dont. \n\t2. detail: **i usually have** ....., **and sometimes** .... **if i have time** - i drink water everyday\n\t3. reason / example: like/ for example today i drunk ...\n\t4. ask back:  **so what about you?** / **so how about you? / how is it different from you?**\n\t5. closing: okay, so have a nice day there / oh wow, okay, so see you around / \n    \nPAIRING: \nA: HỎI \nB: TRẢ LỜI -> HỎI LẠI \nA: TRẢ LỜI -> CHÚC BUỔI SÁNG / NGÀY TỐT \n### PPJ small talk\n\n3. Do you usually check your emails first thing in the morning?\n    \n4. Do you normally plan your tasks before you start working?\n    \n\n### Follow-up\n\n- Why?\n    \n- What about today?\n    \n- Has that always been your routine?\n    \n\n# 3. TYPE 2 — HOW OFTEN…?\n\n## Frequency\n\n### Answer pattern\n\n> I usually… about twice a week.  :: \n> **I usually + do smthing + about + .... times a day/ .... times a week /... times a  month /** \n> Not very often, actually. Maybe once or twice a month.\n\n### Daily small talk\n\n1. How often do you go **window-shopping**? (đi xem nhưng không mua)\n\t1. i do that almost everyday, thru shoppee and lazada \n\t2. yet i dont usually buy them, only until i receive my salary\n\t3. how about you?\n    -> **A- TRẢ LỜI:** \n\t\t\t\t**1. Oh, me too**\n\t\t\t\t**2. oh, that's so me**\n\t\t\t\t**3. oh, likewise** \n\t\t\t\t**4. no way, me too** \n\t\t\t\t**5. same here, do you believe it?**\n2. How often do you eat out?\n    \n\n### PPJ small talk\n\n3. How often do you work with other departments?\n    \n4. How often do you need to follow up with suppliers?\n    \n\n### Useful frequency language\n\n| Frequency          | Natural English    |\n| ------------------ | ------------------ |\n| 100%               | almost every day   |\n| thường xuyên       | pretty regularly   |\n| đôi khi            | every now and then |\n| không thường xuyên | not that often     |\n| hiếm               | once in a while    |\n| gần như không      | hardly ever        |\n\n# 4. TYPE 3 — WHAT DO YOU USUALLY…?\n\n## Routine\n\n### Answer pattern\n\n> I usually start by…  \n> After that, I normally…\n\n### Daily small talk\n\n1. What do you usually do after work?\n\t1. \n    \n2. What do you usually do on **Sunday mornings**?\n\t1. answer: **i would** go to the market and buy fresh food(...), **then** we go to the theme park\n\t2. details:\n\t3. reason / example:\n\t4. ask back: **is it the same with you?** **so what about you?** / **so how about you? / how is it different from you?**\n\t/ respone: \n\t\t\t\t**1. Oh, me too**\n\t\t\t\t**2. oh, that's so me**\n\t\t\t\t**3. oh, likewise** \n\t\t\t\t**4. no way, me too** \n\t\t\t\t**5. same here, do you believe it?**\n    \n\n### PPJ small talk\n\n3. **What do you usually do when you arrive at work?**\n- (answer) - i check my email first \n\t\t- i check my pending tasks\n\t\t- i call ... ( the supplier)\n\t\t- i report to ...\n\t\t- i create .... \n\t\t- i handle \n- (details) - i need to update the information before starting work \n\t\t- i want to make sure ....\n\t\t- i'm hoping to receive ... \n\t\t- i need to obtain / receive directions from my boss\n- (example / reason)\n- (ask back) -> so how's about you ? \n\t- so what about you?\n\t- so how is it going by you?\n\t- so whats different from yours?\n    \n3. **What do you normally do when you receive an urgent request?**\n    - (answer) - i check my email first \n\t\t- i check my pending tasks\n\t\t- i call ... ( the supplier)\n\t\t- i report to ...\n\t\t- i create .... \n\t\t- i handle \n- (details) - i need to update the information before starting work \n\t\t- i want to make sure ....\n\t\t- i'm hoping to receive ... \n\t\t- i need to obtain / receive directions from my boss\n- (example / reason)\n- (ask back) -> so how's about you ? \n\t- so what about you?\n\t- so how is it going by you?\n\t- so whats different from yours?\n\n# 5. TYPE 4 — WHAT KIND OF…?\n\n## Category / Type\n\n### Answer pattern\n\n> I’m mostly into…  + CATEGORY + \n> \t\t\t\t\t\t\t+ TIMES THAT MAKE YOU START THAT HOBBY / PREFERENCES / LIKING\n> \t\t\t\t\t\t\t+ WITH SOMEBODY -> \n> \t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t  + ALONG WITH SOMEBODY \n> \t\t\t\t\t\t\t+ WHEN -\n> \t\t\t\t\t\t\t+ WHERE\n> I tend to prefer… because…\n\n### Daily small talk\n\n1. What kind of music do you usually listen to?\n> I’m mostly into / i like / i love / i enjoy / i usually listen to  …  + CATEGORY + ///// IM MOSTLY INTO TECHNO AND SOMETIMES CLASSICAL MUSIC\n> \t\t\t\t\t\t\t+ **TIMES THAT MAKE YOU START THAT HOBBY / PREFERENCES / LIKING //** I HAVE BEEN LISTENING TO CLASSICAL MUSIC SINCE ELEMENTARY SCHOOL, BUT IT WAS JUST A FEW YEARS BACK THAT I STARTED PLAYING TECHNO\n> \t\t\t\t\t\t\t+ **WITH SOMEBODY** -> I'M MOSTLY INTO POP MUSIC AND LISTEN TO LATEST SONGS WITH MY FRIEND\n> \t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t  + **FROM SOMEBODY**  - I'M MOSTLY INTO POP MUSIC, ESPECIALLY FROM GIA AND DOJA CAT \n> \t\t\t\t\t\t\t+ **WHEN -** im mostly into **folk, soul and indie** music, i listen to them when i'm alone, its helps me to relax and feel comforted\n> \t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t+ im mostly into meditation and healing music, especially before i go to sleep, or whenever i feel tired\n> \t\t\t\t\t\t\t+ **WHERE** - i'm mostly into **R&B, ballad** music when i take shower at home, but when i work i have to listen to ambient sounds \n> \t\t\t\t\t\t\t+ \n    \n2. What kind of movies do you enjoy?\n    \n\nA: HỎI \nB: TRẢ LỜI -> HỎI LẠI \nA: TRẢ LỜI -> CHÚC BUỔI SÁNG / NGÀY TỐT / DUA RA 1 LOI DE NGHI\n### PPJ small talk\n\n3. What kind of tasks take up most of your time?\n    \n4. What kind of problems do you usually deal with at work?\n    \n\n### Natural chunks\n\n- I’m mostly into…\n    \n- I tend to prefer…\n    \n- It really depends.\n    \n- Mostly…\n    \n- Anything related to…\n    \n\n# 6. TYPE 5 — WHAT IS YOUR FAVORITE…?\n\n## Favourite / Preference\n\n### Answer pattern\n\n>  I’d say…  / Probably…  (answer)\n> I would definitely do smthing + in some place + with somebody (detail)\n Mainly because… (reason)\n ask back \n\n### Daily small talk\n\n1. What is your favorite time of a day?\n    >  I’d say the **evening**  / Probably the evening  (answer)\n> \tI would definitely have much more time for my gf/family/friends \n\t Mainly because **this is the only time that we have free-time together** to reconnect and update each other on our days (reason)\n ask back -> so whats about you?\n // update somebody on our days: (phrasal.verb) cap nhat ai do ve nhung gi dien ra trong ngay cua minh\n\n- \n \n2. What **is your favorite way** to relax?\n\t1. **i would say** *listening to music* **is my favorite** way to relax \n\t\t1. detail: \n\t\t\t1. when \n\t\t\t\t- ESPECIALLY WHEN I ARRIVE AT WORK TO START A NEW DAY \n\t\t\t\t ESPECIALLY WHEN I TAKE A SHOWER AND GET READY TO GO TO BED // \n\t\t\t\t ESPECIALLY WHEN I WAKE UP EARLY IN THE MORNING - \n\t\t\t\t ESPECIALLY WHEN I DRINK COFFEE IN THE MORNING\n\t\t\t2. WHERE \n\t\t\t\t- ESPECIALLY WHEN I'M AT HOME / \n\t\t\t\t- ESPECIALLY WHEN I HAVE TIME TO GO TO THEATER AND ENJOY MUSIC \n\t\t\t3. WITH WHOM \n\t\t\t\t- WITH MY FAMILY \n\t\t\t\t- WITH MY FRIENDS \n\t\t\t\t- WITH MY COLLEAGUES \n\t\t\t\t- WITH MY ...\n\t\t2. example / reason: \n\t2. i would say playing pickleball with my team \n\t\t1. detail:\n\t\t2. example / reason: \n\t3. i would say **drinking alcohol** (beer) and **do karaoke** with my family and friends\n\t4. i would say going to cafes with my family and friends is my favorite way to relax\n\t5. i would say watching movies with my family and friends is my favorite way to relax\n    \n\n### PPJ small talk\n\n3. What is your favorite part of your job?\n    \n4. What type of task do you enjoy doing the most?\n    \n\n> **Natural upgrade:**  \n> Instead of always saying **“My favorite is…”**, use:\n> \n> **Probably…**  \n> **I’d probably go with…**  \n> **If I had to choose, I’d say…**\n\n# 7. TYPE 6 — WHICH DO YOU PREFER…?\n\n## Choice\n\n### Answer pattern\n\n> I’d probably choose A over B because…  \n> It depends, but generally I prefer…\n\n### Daily small talk\n\n1. Which do you prefer, staying home or going out?\n    \n2. Which do you prefer, mornings or evenings?\n    \n\n### PPJ small talk\n\n3. Which do you prefer, discussing an issue by message or face-to-face?\n    \n4. Which do you prefer, working independently or working with a team?\n    \n\n# 8. TYPE 7 — WOULD YOU RATHER…?\n\n## Choice / Hypothetical Preference\n\n### Answer pattern\n\n> I’d rather…  \n> Mainly because…\n\n### Daily small talk\n\n1. Would you rather travel alone or with friends?\n    \n2. Would you rather have more free time or more money?\n    \n\n### PPJ small talk\n\n3. Would you rather handle one large project or several smaller tasks?\n    \n4. Would you rather receive instructions by email or in a meeting?\n    \n\n# 9. TYPE 8 — WHY…?\n\n## Reason\n\n### Answer pattern\n\n> Mainly because…  \n> I think the main reason is…\n\n### Daily small talk\n\n1. Why do you enjoy weekends?\n    \n2. Why do you think people enjoy travelling?\n    \n\n### PPJ small talk\n\n3. Why is communication important at work?\n    \n4. Why do misunderstandings sometimes happen between departments?\n    \n\n### Reason bank\n\n- Mainly because…\n    \n- One reason is that…\n    \n- I guess it’s because…\n    \n- Probably because…\n    \n- The main reason would be…\n    \n\n# 10. TYPE 9 — WHEN…?\n\n## Time\n\n### Answer pattern\n\n> Usually…  \n> Most of the time…  \n> I normally… when…\n\n### Daily small talk\n\n1. When do you feel most productive?\n    \n2. When do you normally relax during the day?\n    \n\n### PPJ small talk\n\n3. When is your busiest time at work?\n    \n4. When do you usually contact another department for support?\n    \n\n# 11. TYPE 10 — WHERE…?\n\n## Place\n\n### Answer pattern\n\n> Usually at/in…  \n> I normally… there because…\n\n### Daily small talk\n\n1. Where do you usually go when you want to relax?\n    \n2. Where do you normally have lunch?\n    \n\n### PPJ small talk\n\n3. Where do you usually have meetings?\n    \n4. Where do you normally get the information you need for your work?\n    \n\n# 12. TYPE 11 — WHO…?\n\n## People\n\n### Answer pattern\n\n> Usually…  \n> I work quite closely with…  \n> The person I normally go to is…\n\n### Daily small talk\n\n1. Who do you spend most of your free time with?\n    \n2. Who do you usually talk to when you have a problem?\n    \n\n### PPJ small talk\n\n3. Who do you work with most often?\n    \n4. Who do you normally contact when there is an urgent issue?\n    \n\n### PPJ extension\n\n> I work quite closely with Accounting and Warehouse because a lot of my tasks require information from both teams.\n\n# 13. TYPE 12 — HOW…?\n\n## Method / Process / Feeling\n\n### Answer pattern\n\n> I usually…  \n> Normally, I start by…\n\n### Daily small talk\n\n1. How do you normally spend your evenings?\n    \n2. How do you relax after a stressful day?\n    \n\n### PPJ small talk\n\n3. How do you normally organize your workload?\n    \n4. How do you handle an urgent request?\n    \n\n### Useful process pattern\n\n> First, I…  \n> Then I…  \n> If necessary, I…\n\n# 14. TYPE 13 — HOW MUCH / HOW MANY…?\n\n## Quantity / Amount\n\n### Daily small talk\n\n1. How much free time do you normally have on weekdays?\n    \n2. How many hours do you usually sleep?\n    \n\n### PPJ small talk\n\n3. How many tasks are you working on at the moment?\n    \n4. How much time do you normally spend checking documents?\n    \n\n### Natural approximation\n\nIELTS không yêu cầu số chính xác.\n\nUse:\n\n- around…\n    \n- roughly…\n    \n- about…\n    \n- probably…\n    \n- somewhere between…\n    \n- quite a lot\n    \n- not that much\n    \n\n# 15. TYPE 14 — DID YOU…?\n\n## Specific Past\n\n### Answer pattern\n\n> Yes, I did. I…  \n> Actually, no. I…\n\n### Daily small talk\n\n1. Did you do anything interesting last weekend?\n    \n2. Did you watch anything good recently?\n    \n\n### PPJ small talk\n\n3. Did you have a busy day yesterday?\n    \n4. Did you deal with any unexpected problems this week?\n    \n\n### Expansion\n\n> Yeah, actually I did. We had a small issue with some documents yesterday, but we managed to sort it out before the deadline.\n\n# 16. TYPE 15 — HAVE YOU EVER…?\n\n## Life Experience\n\n### Answer pattern\n\n> Yeah, a few times.  \n> Actually, I have.  \n> Not yet, but I’d like to.\n\n### Daily small talk\n\n1. Have you ever travelled alone?\n    \n2. Have you ever tried learning a completely new skill?\n    \n\n### PPJ small talk\n\n3. Have you ever worked with an overseas supplier?\n    \n4. Have you ever had to solve a serious problem under time pressure?\n    \n\n### Important distinction\n\n**Did you…?** → thời điểm/quá khứ cụ thể.\n\n> Did you work late yesterday?\n\n**Have you ever…?** → trải nghiệm trong đời, không cần thời điểm cụ thể.\n\n> Have you ever worked overnight?\n\n# 17. TYPE 16 — HAS… CHANGED?\n\n## Past vs Present / Change\n\n### Answer pattern\n\n> Definitely. It used to…, but now…  \n> Not really. It’s pretty much the same.\n\n### Daily small talk\n\n1. Has your daily routine changed in recent years?\n    \n2. Has the way you spend your free time changed?\n    \n\n### PPJ small talk\n\n3. Has your job changed since you first joined the company?\n    \n4. Has technology changed the way your team works?\n    \n\n### Strong speaking pattern\n\n> **It used to be… → but now… → because…**\n\n> We used to handle most of the process manually, but now more tasks are done through the system, so the process is much faster.\n\n# 18. TYPE 17 — DO YOU THINK…?\n\n## Opinion\n\n### Answer pattern\n\n> Yeah, I think so.  \n> Personally, I think…  \n> It depends, but generally…\n\n### Daily small talk\n\n1. Do you think people have enough free time nowadays?\n    \n2. Do you think hobbies are important?\n    \n\n### PPJ small talk\n\n3. Do you think small talk is important at work?\n    \n4. Do you think technology makes communication easier?\n    \n\n### IELTS rule\n\nKhông biến Part 1 thành debate dài.\n\n**Opinion + one reason + optional example.**\n\n# 19. TYPE 18 — HOW IMPORTANT IS…?\n\n## Importance\n\n### Answer pattern\n\n> Pretty important, actually.  \n> I’d say it’s really important because…\n\n### Daily small talk\n\n1. How important is having free time to you?\n    \n2. How important is music in your daily life?\n    \n\n### PPJ small talk\n\n3. How important is teamwork in your department?\n    \n4. How important is English for your work?\n    \n\n# 20. TYPE 19 — WOULD YOU LIKE TO…?\n\n## Future Desire\n\n### Answer pattern\n\n> Yeah, definitely. I’d like to…  \n> Probably not. I’d rather…\n\n### Daily small talk\n\n1. Would you like to travel more in the future?\n    \n2. Would you like to learn another language?\n    \n\n### PPJ small talk\n\n3. Would you like to work on more international projects?\n    \n4. Would you like to use English more often at work?\n    \n\n# 21. TYPE 20 — WHAT ARE YOU GOING TO…?\n\n## Near Future / Plan\n\n### Daily small talk\n\n1. What are you going to do this weekend?\n    \n2. What are you planning to do after work today?\n    \n\n### PPJ small talk\n\n3. What are you going to work on this afternoon?\n    \n4. What tasks are you planning to finish this week?\n    \n\n### Natural future bank\n\n- I’m planning to…\n    \n- I’m hoping to…\n    \n- I need to…\n    \n- I’ll probably…\n    \n- I haven’t decided yet.\n    \n\n# 22. TYPE 21 — WHAT DO YOU LIKE / DISLIKE ABOUT…?\n\n## Evaluation\n\n### Daily small talk\n\n1. What do you like about your neighborhood?\n    \n2. What do you dislike about commuting?\n    \n\n### PPJ small talk\n\n3. What do you like most about your department?\n    \n4. What do you find difficult about cross-department communication?\n    \n\n### Natural pattern\n\n> What I like most is…  \n> The only thing I don’t really like is…\n\n# 23. TYPE 22 — IS / ARE… COMMON?\n\n## Description / General Situation\n\n### Daily small talk\n\n1. Is eating out common among your friends?\n    \n2. Are weekend trips popular where you live?\n    \n\n### PPJ small talk\n\n3. Are urgent requests common in your department?\n    \n4. Is overtime common during busy periods?\n    \n\n### Answer pattern\n\n> Yeah, quite common actually.  \n> Not really. It happens occasionally, especially when…\n\n# 24. TYPE 23 — WHAT WAS THE LAST…?\n\n## Recent Experience\n\n### Daily small talk\n\n1. What was the last movie you watched?\n    \n2. What was the last place you visited?\n    \n\n### PPJ small talk\n\n3. What was the last problem you solved at work?\n    \n4. What was the last department you worked with?\n    \n\nThis format is particularly useful for **real small talk** because the answer naturally becomes a short story.\n\n# 25. TYPE 24 — HOW IS X DIFFERENT FROM Y?\n\n## Comparison\n\n### Daily small talk\n\n1. How are your weekends different from your weekdays?\n    \n2. How is your life now different from five years ago?\n    \n\n### PPJ small talk\n\n3. How is a normal workday different from a busy workday for you?\n    \n4. How is communicating with suppliers different from communicating with colleagues?\n    \n\n### Pattern\n\n> A is usually…, whereas B…  \n> The main difference is…\n\n# 26. PPJ RAPID-FIRE SMALL TALK BANK\n\nTeacher asks randomly. Student has **3 seconds to start speaking**.\n\n|#|Question|\n|--:|---|\n|1|How’s your day going so far?|\n|2|Has it been a busy morning?|\n|3|What are you working on today?|\n|4|What’s keeping you busy this week?|\n|5|Have you had lunch yet?|\n|6|How was your commute today?|\n|7|Did you have a busy day yesterday?|\n|8|Do you have anything urgent today?|\n|9|What’s your main priority today?|\n|10|Have you got anything pending?|\n|11|Who are you working with today?|\n|12|Which department do you work with most?|\n|13|Have you had any supplier issues recently?|\n|14|Did anything unexpected come up today?|\n|15|How do you normally start your workday?|\n|16|What kind of tasks take up most of your time?|\n|17|How often do you attend meetings?|\n|18|What part of your job do you enjoy most?|\n|19|What part of your job is the most challenging?|\n|20|Has your workload changed recently?|\n|21|Are you working on anything new at the moment?|\n|22|Would you like to learn a new skill this year?|\n|23|Do you think English is becoming more important at work?|\n|24|What are you hoping to finish this week?|\n|25|What was one small problem you solved recently?|\n\n# 27. FOLLOW-UP REFLEX BANK\n\nĐây mới là phần biến **IELTS practice → real conversation**. Chapter 1 và 2 của _Compelling Conversations_ cũng nhấn mạnh việc khuyến khích người nói tiếp tục bằng các responses và follow-up questions.\n\n|Trigger|Natural Follow-up|\n|---|---|\n|Nghe thú vị|Really?|\n|Muốn biết thêm|Tell me more about that.|\n|Hỏi lý do|How come?|\n|Hỏi chi tiết|What was that like?|\n|Hỏi kết quả|How did it go?|\n|Hỏi tiếp diễn|What happened next?|\n|Hỏi tần suất|Does that happen often?|\n|Hỏi hiện tại|What about now?|\n|So với trước|Was it always like that?|\n|Hỏi người liên quan|Who did you work with?|\n|Hỏi cách xử lý|How did you handle it?|\n|Hỏi cảm nhận|How did you feel about that?|\n\n# 28. THE 5 GOLDEN ANSWER PATTERNS\n\nHọc viên không cần học 24 công thức. Chỉ cần phản xạ được 5 pattern này.\n\n### Pattern A — Habit\n\n> I usually…  \n> Most of the time…  \n> because…\n\n### Pattern B — Preference\n\n> I prefer…  \n> mainly because…\n\n### Pattern C — Past experience\n\n> Yeah, actually…  \n> Last…  \n> It was…\n\n### Pattern D — Change\n\n> It used to be…  \n> but now…  \n> because…\n\n### Pattern E — Opinion\n\n> Personally, I think…  \n> mainly because…  \n> For example…\n\n# 29. 10-MINUTE DAILY SPEAKING PREP\n\n### Minute 0–2: Rapid Fire\n\nAnswer **5 random questions**.\n\nMaximum preparation: **3 seconds/question**.\n\n### Minute 2–5: Expand\n\nChoose 3 questions.\n\nSpeak **20–30 seconds each**.\n\n### Minute 5–7: Follow-up\n\nPartner asks:\n\n> Why?  \n> How come?  \n> What about now?  \n> What happened next?\n\n### Minute 7–9: PPJ Switch\n\nTake one normal question and convert it into workplace English.\n\n> How often do you exercise?  \n> → How often do you attend meetings?\n\n> What do you normally do in the morning?  \n> → What do you normally do when you arrive at work?\n\n### Minute 9–10: No-preparation challenge\n\nTeacher asks one unseen question.\n\nStudent must:\n\n**Start within 3 seconds → give 2–4 sentences → finish naturally.**\n\n# 30. STUDENT TARGET\n\nBy the end of the drill, you should be able to:\n\n- answer without translating the whole sentence into Vietnamese first\n    \n- start speaking within 2–3 seconds\n    \n- give 2–4 connected sentences\n    \n- use present, past and future naturally\n    \n- explain habits, preferences, experiences and opinions\n    \n- ask at least one follow-up question\n    \n- move naturally between **daily chit-chat → workplace small talk**\n    \n- keep a conversation going instead of simply answering questions\n    \n\n## Core rule\n\n> **QUESTION → ANSWER → DETAIL → WHY / EXAMPLE → FOLLOW-UP**\n\nKhông học để “trả bài IELTS”.\n\nHọc để khi đồng nghiệp hỏi:\n\n> **“Busy morning?”**\n\nmình không chỉ nói:\n\n> **“Yes.”**\n\nmà có thể phản xạ:\n\n> **“Yeah, pretty busy actually. I’ve been checking a few pending documents since this morning, and I still have one supplier issue to follow up on. How about you?”**","x":17080,"y":9760,"width":1040,"height":19288},
		{"id":"e2ef13841e1e6d51","type":"text","text":"# Natural Chit-Chat Flow\n\n## Daily Life + PPJ Workplace Small Talk\n\nMục tiêu là biến các dạng câu hỏi IELTS Speaking Part 1 thành **một cuộc trò chuyện thật**, thay vì hỏi liên tục như interview.\n\nTinh thần này cũng đúng với _Compelling Conversations_: câu hỏi nên là **conversation starters**, sau đó người nói phản hồi, hỏi thêm và phát triển câu chuyện tự nhiên. Phần worksheet của khóa PPJ cũng ưu tiên practical workplace English, confidence và natural expressions cho adult learners.\n\n## 1. The Natural Conversation Formula\n\nĐừng dùng:\n\n> Question → Answer → Question → Answer → Question\n\nDùng:\n\n> **Opener → Answer → Reaction → Follow-up → Small story → Return question**\n\nVí dụ:\n\n**A:** How’s your day going?  \n**B:** Pretty good. A bit busy, though.  \n**A:** Yeah? What’s keeping you busy?  \n**B:** I’ve got a couple of reports to finish before lunch.  \n**A:** Ah, got it. Is that something you do every week?  \n**B:** Pretty much. How about you? Busy today?\n\nĐây là flow cần luyện.\n\n# 2. FLOW A — VERY LIGHT OPENER\n\nDùng khi vừa gặp nhau, trước giờ học, trong pantry, lift, corridor, trước meeting.\n\n|Step|Daily Life|PPJ Workplace|\n|---|---|---|\n|Open|How’s your day going?|How’s your morning going?|\n|Easy follow-up|Been busy today?|Busy morning?|\n|Detail|What have you been up to?|What are you working on today?|\n|Reaction|Sounds good. / Nice.|Ah, got it. / Sounds busy.|\n|Return|How about you?|What about your side?|\n\n### Natural example\n\n**A:** Morning. How’s it going?  \n**B:** Not bad. Pretty relaxed so far.  \n**A:** Nice. Got much going on today?  \n**B:** Just a few things. I need to finish a report this afternoon.  \n**A:** Sounds manageable.  \n**B:** Hopefully. How about you?\n\n# 3. FLOW B — MORNING / DAILY ROUTINE\n\nIELTS question types hidden inside the conversation:\n\n- What do you usually…?\n    \n- How often…?\n    \n- Do you normally…?\n    \n- What time…?\n    \n- Has your routine changed?\n    \n\n### Daily Life\n\n**A:** Are you a morning person?  \n**B:** Not really. I need some time to wake up properly.  \n**A:** Same here. Do you usually have coffee?  \n**B:** Yeah, almost every morning.  \n**A:** At home or on the way to work?  \n**B:** Usually at home. It’s kind of part of my routine now.\n\n### PPJ\n\n**A:** What do you usually check first when you get in?  \n**B:** Normally my emails and Teams messages.  \n**A:** Same. Do you usually have a lot waiting for you in the morning?  \n**B:** It depends. Mondays are normally the busiest.  \n**A:** Yeah, Mondays are rough.\n\n# 4. FLOW C — “WHAT ARE YOU UP TO?”\n\nĐây là một trong những opener tự nhiên nhất.\n\n### Daily\n\n> What are you up to after work?\n\n> Got anything planned tonight?\n\n> Doing anything this weekend?\n\n> Any plans for lunch?\n\n### PPJ\n\n> What are you working on today?\n\n> Anything urgent on your side?\n\n> Got anything big coming up this week?\n\n> What’s keeping you busy at the moment?\n\n### Sample\n\n**A:** Got anything planned after work?  \n**B:** Probably just dinner and Netflix. Nothing exciting.  \n**A:** Honestly, that sounds pretty good. What are you watching?  \n**B:** I just started a new crime series.  \n**A:** Any good?  \n**B:** Yeah, surprisingly good.\n\nNotice:\n\n**Question → opinion → follow-up**, not interview.\n\n# 5. FLOW D — FOOD / COFFEE / LUNCH\n\nRất safe cho workplace small talk.\n\n### Openers\n\n- Had lunch yet?\n    \n- What did you have?\n    \n- Where do you normally eat?\n    \n- Do you usually bring lunch or eat out?\n    \n- Coffee or tea?\n    \n- How many coffees have you had today?\n    \n\n### PPJ mini-dialogue\n\n**A:** Had lunch yet?  \n**B:** Not yet. I’ve been stuck in meetings all morning.  \n**A:** Oh no. Are you going out or eating here?  \n**B:** Probably here. I don’t really have much time today.  \n**A:** Same. It’s been one of those days.\n\n### Useful natural phrase\n\n> **It’s been one of those days.**\n\n= Hôm nay đúng kiểu một ngày nhiều chuyện / khá mệt / nhiều việc.\n\n# 6. FLOW E — WEEKEND SMALL TALK\n\n### Before the weekend\n\n> Got any plans for the weekend?\n\n> Doing anything interesting?\n\n> Are you going anywhere?\n\n> Planning to just take it easy?\n\n### After the weekend\n\n> How was your weekend?\n\n> Do anything interesting?\n\n> Did you go anywhere?\n\n> Get a chance to relax?\n\n### Natural flow\n\n**A:** How was your weekend?  \n**B:** Pretty good. I went to Bảo Lộc with some friends.  \n**A:** Nice. Was it your first time there?  \n**B:** No, I’ve been there a few times. I just like the weather.  \n**A:** Yeah, it’s much cooler up there. What did you guys do?\n\nThis naturally trains:\n\n- Simple Past\n    \n- Present Perfect\n    \n- preferences\n    \n- description\n    \n- follow-up questions\n    \n\n# 7. FLOW F — WORKLOAD SMALL TALK\n\nKhông bắt đầu quá formal kiểu:\n\n> “What is your current workload?”\n\nThay bằng:\n\n- Busy today?\n    \n- Got a lot on?\n    \n- How’s your workload this week?\n    \n- What’s keeping you busy?\n    \n- Is it a busy period for your team?\n    \n- Things calming down yet?\n    \n\n### PPJ example\n\n**A:** Busy week?  \n**B:** Yeah, quite busy actually. We’ve got several orders to check.  \n**A:** Is it because of month-end?  \n**B:** Partly. We’ve also got some pending supplier documents.  \n**A:** Ah, got it. Hopefully things calm down next week.\n\n# 8. FLOW G — CURRENT TASK → NATURAL WORK TALK\n\n### Start light\n\n> What are you working on today?\n\n### Then expand\n\n> How’s it going?\n\n> Is it a big task?\n\n> Who are you working with on that?\n\n> Is that something you handle regularly?\n\n> When do you need to finish it?\n\n### Example\n\n**A:** What are you working on today?  \n**B:** I’m checking some invoice data.  \n**A:** Oh, is there an issue?  \n**B:** Just a small mismatch between the invoice and PO.  \n**A:** Got it. Have you figured out what caused it?  \n**B:** Not yet. I’m checking with Purchasing now.\n\nNatural workplace speaking without becoming a formal meeting.\n\n# 9. FLOW H — SMALL PROBLEM, LIGHT TONE\n\nKhông phải mọi problem conversation đều cần “professional issue escalation”.\n\n### Useful openings\n\n- Everything okay?\n    \n- You look busy.\n    \n- Something come up?\n    \n- Having one of those days?\n    \n- Any issues today?\n    \n- Did something go wrong?\n    \n\n### PPJ example\n\n**A:** You look busy. Everything okay?  \n**B:** Yeah, just dealing with a delivery issue.  \n**A:** Ah. Late delivery?  \n**B:** Yeah. The supplier pushed it back again.  \n**A:** That’s frustrating. Is it affecting production?  \n**B:** Not yet, luckily.\n\n# 10. FLOW I — CROSS-DEPARTMENT SMALL TALK\n\nMột flow rất hữu ích tại PPJ.\n\n**A:** Which team are you working with today?  \n**B:** Mostly Warehouse and Purchasing.  \n**A:** Oh, what are you working on together?  \n**B:** We’re checking a quantity mismatch.  \n**A:** Does that happen often?  \n**B:** Not that often, but when it does, it takes time to trace everything.  \n**A:** Yeah, especially when several systems are involved.\n\n# 11. FLOW J — TECHNOLOGY / AI / SYSTEM SMALL TALK\n\n### Questions\n\n- Have you tried the new system yet?\n    \n- How are you finding it?\n    \n- Is it easier than the old process?\n    \n- Do you use AI much at work?\n    \n- What do you normally use it for?\n    \n- Has it saved you much time?\n    \n- Is there anything you still prefer doing manually?\n    \n\n### Example\n\n**A:** Have you tried the new tool yet?  \n**B:** Yeah, a couple of times.  \n**A:** How are you finding it?  \n**B:** Pretty useful actually. It saves me a lot of repetitive work.  \n**A:** Nice. What do you mainly use it for?  \n**B:** Mostly checking information and drafting summaries.\n\n# 12. FLOW K — TALKING ABOUT PEOPLE WITHOUT GOSSIP\n\nSafe workplace small talk:\n\n- Who do you work with most often?\n    \n- Which department do you usually coordinate with?\n    \n- Do you prefer working independently or with a team?\n    \n- Who normally handles that part?\n    \n- Have you worked with their team before?\n    \n- Is your team quite busy at the moment?\n    \n\nAvoid:\n\n> Why is he always late?\n\nBetter:\n\n> Has their team been particularly busy recently?\n\n# 13. FLOW L — ENDING THE CONVERSATION NATURALLY\n\nStudents often know how to start but not how to leave.\n\n### Daily\n\n- Anyway, I’d better get going.\n    \n- I’ll let you get back to it.\n    \n- Catch you later.\n    \n- Have a good one.\n    \n- Enjoy your lunch.\n    \n- Have a good weekend.\n    \n\n### PPJ\n\n- Anyway, I’ll let you get back to work.\n    \n- I’d better get back to this.\n    \n- Catch up with you later.\n    \n- Let me know how it goes.\n    \n- Hope everything goes smoothly.\n    \n- See you in the meeting later.\n    \n\n# 14. NATURAL REACTION BANK\n\nĐây là phần cực quan trọng để conversation không biến thành Q&A.\n\n|Situation|Natural reaction|\n|---|---|\n|Interesting|Oh, really?|\n|Understand|Ah, got it.|\n|Same experience|Same here.|\n|Agree|Exactly.|\n|Mild surprise|Seriously?|\n|Positive|Nice.|\n|Good result|That’s good.|\n|Difficult situation|That sounds rough.|\n|Busy|Sounds busy.|\n|Understand problem|That makes sense.|\n|Lucky situation|Luckily!|\n|Hope|Hopefully.|\n|Empathy|Yeah, I can imagine.|\n|Small problem|That’s annoying.|\n|Positive encouragement|Sounds good.|\n\n# 15. FOLLOW-UP QUESTION BANK\n\n|Function|Follow-up|\n|---|---|\n|Ask reason|How come?|\n|More detail|What happened?|\n|Continue story|What happened next?|\n|Experience|Have you done that before?|\n|Frequency|Does that happen often?|\n|Person|Who were you working with?|\n|Process|How did you handle it?|\n|Result|How did it go?|\n|Feeling|How did you feel about it?|\n|Current status|Is it sorted now?|\n|Comparison|Is it normally like that?|\n|Future|What are you going to do next?|\n\n# 16. VOCAB + COLLOCATION BANK\n\n## Daily Life + PPJ Small Talk\n\n|Vocabulary|Nghĩa tiếng Việt|5 Useful Collocations|\n|---|---|---|\n|**routine**|thói quen / lịch sinh hoạt|daily routine; morning routine; work routine; usual routine; stick to a routine|\n|**commute**|việc di chuyển đi làm|daily commute; morning commute; long commute; short commute; commute to work|\n|**plan**|kế hoạch|make plans; weekend plans; change plans; have plans; stick to the plan|\n|**relax**|thư giãn|relax at home; relax after work; relax for a while; completely relax; help me relax|\n|**unwind**|thư giãn sau khi căng thẳng|unwind after work; time to unwind; help me unwind; unwind at home; relax and unwind|\n|**catch up**|cập nhật / trò chuyện sau một thời gian|catch up with friends; catch up over coffee; catch up on work; catch up later; quick catch-up|\n|**workload**|khối lượng công việc|heavy workload; manage workload; current workload; workload increases; reduce workload|\n|**busy**|bận|busy morning; busy week; really busy; keep busy; busy with work|\n|**hectic**|rất bận, dồn dập|hectic day; hectic week; hectic schedule; pretty hectic; things get hectic|\n|**priority**|việc ưu tiên|top priority; main priority; set priorities; current priority; high priority|\n|**pending**|đang chờ xử lý|pending task; pending approval; pending document; pending request; still pending|\n|**deadline**|hạn chót|meet a deadline; tight deadline; upcoming deadline; miss a deadline; extend the deadline|\n|**follow up**|theo dõi / hỏi lại|follow up with someone; follow up on something; follow-up email; follow-up call; need to follow up|\n|**sort out**|giải quyết / xử lý ổn thỏa|sort out an issue; sort out the problem; get it sorted out; sort things out; help sort it out|\n|**issue**|vấn đề|small issue; technical issue; supplier issue; resolve an issue; run into an issue|\n|**mismatch**|sự không khớp|quantity mismatch; data mismatch; invoice mismatch; identify a mismatch; check the mismatch|\n|**urgent**|khẩn cấp|urgent task; urgent request; urgent issue; quite urgent; something urgent|\n|**tied up**|đang bận không rảnh|tied up in a meeting; tied up with work; tied up all morning; currently tied up; a bit tied up|\n|**wrap up**|hoàn tất|wrap up a task; wrap up the meeting; wrap things up; wrap up for the day; quickly wrap up|\n|**smoothly**|suôn sẻ|go smoothly; run smoothly; work smoothly; everything went smoothly; make things run smoothly|\n\n# 17. FIVE HIGH-VALUE COLLOCATION SETS\n\nHọc viên không cần nhớ từng từ riêng lẻ. Học theo **chunks**.\n\n### BUSY\n\n- busy morning\n    \n- busy week\n    \n- pretty busy\n    \n- busy with work\n    \n- keep someone busy\n    \n\n> It’s been a **pretty busy week**.\n\n### WORKLOAD\n\n- heavy workload\n    \n- manage my workload\n    \n- current workload\n    \n- workload has increased\n    \n- balance my workload\n    \n\n> My **workload has increased** a bit this month.\n\n### ISSUE\n\n- run into an issue\n    \n- deal with an issue\n    \n- resolve an issue\n    \n- supplier issue\n    \n- minor issue\n    \n\n> We **ran into a small issue** with the delivery.\n\n### FOLLOW UP\n\n- follow up on something\n    \n- follow up with someone\n    \n- follow-up email\n    \n- follow-up call\n    \n- need to follow up\n    \n\n> I need to **follow up with the supplier** this afternoon.\n\n### SORT OUT\n\n- sort out the issue\n    \n- sort out the problem\n    \n- get it sorted\n    \n- sort things out\n    \n- help me sort this out\n    \n\n> It’s okay. We’ve **got it sorted now**.\n\n# 18. LIGHTER ALTERNATIVES TO FORMAL WORKPLACE ENGLISH\n\n|Formal / stiff|Natural chit-chat|\n|---|---|\n|How is your workload today?|Busy today?|\n|What tasks are you currently performing?|What are you working on?|\n|Have you encountered any issues?|Any issues so far?|\n|What is your current priority?|What’s your main priority today?|\n|Have you completed the task?|Finished it yet?|\n|What is the current status?|How’s it going?|\n|Has the issue been resolved?|Is it sorted now?|\n|What caused the problem?|Do you know what happened?|\n|Are you available?|Got a minute?|\n|Shall we discuss this matter?|Want to quickly go over it?|\n|I will contact you later.|I’ll catch up with you later.|\n|I hope the task proceeds successfully.|Hope it goes smoothly.|\n\n# 19. THE PPJ SMALL-TALK LOOP\n\nMột pattern tôi khuyên dùng hằng ngày:\n\n### 1. OPEN\n\n> Busy morning?\n\n### 2. RESPOND\n\n> Yeah, a little. I’ve got a couple of things pending.\n\n### 3. REACT\n\n> Ah, got it.\n\n### 4. FOLLOW\n\n> Anything urgent?\n\n### 5. SHARE\n\n> Mostly supplier documents. I’m waiting for confirmation.\n\n### 6. CONNECT\n\n> Yeah, we’ve been waiting for a few things too.\n\n### 7. RETURN\n\n> How about your team?\n\n### 8. CLOSE\n\n> Anyway, hope it goes smoothly.\n\nĐó là **real conversation rhythm**:\n\n> **Ask → React → Follow → Share → Return**\n\nKhông phải:\n\n> **Ask → Ask → Ask → Ask.**\n\n# 20. DAILY 5-MINUTE CHIT-CHAT DRILL\n\nPair A chọn một opener:\n\n> How’s your day going?\n\nPair B trả lời tối thiểu **2 câu**.\n\nPair A phải:\n\n1. react\n    \n2. ask one follow-up\n    \n3. share something about themselves\n    \n\nPair B hỏi ngược lại.\n\n### Success example\n\n**A:** Busy today?  \n**B:** A little. I’ve got two reports to finish before lunch.  \n**A:** Sounds busy. Is that something you handle every week?  \n**B:** Yeah, pretty much. Mondays are normally the busiest.  \n**A:** Same here. Monday mornings are always hectic for me.  \n**B:** What are you working on today?\n\n**Target:** Sau khoảng 30–45 giây, cả hai người đều đã **ask + answer + react + share**.","x":18680,"y":19520,"width":921,"height":14138},
		{"id":"e3be3f565a095656","type":"text","text":"black coffee ","x":16940,"y":10800,"width":140,"height":60},
		{"id":"bcb24effd2c17b46","type":"text","text":"i usually shoppee it \nand i **scroll shoppee** everyday, putting everything i like in the cart\n- scroll + social media :: lướt cái gì đó ","x":16740,"y":11780,"width":340,"height":140},
		{"id":"b62a58f65dc15b8f","type":"text","text":"![[Pasted image 20260820175619.png]]","x":17880,"y":12060,"width":620,"height":100},
		{"id":"ed7b8e6e80db8ae9","type":"text","text":"homework: \nrecord yourself + your friend chitchating about any questions in type 1-2-3","x":16760,"y":12013,"width":300,"height":147},
		{"id":"57b626fde67c67b0","type":"text","text":"# 10 Short Natural Chit-Chat Dialogues\n\n## Daily Life + PPJ Workplace\n\nCác đoạn dưới đây cố tình mix nhiều dạng câu hỏi IELTS Speaking Part 1 nhưng vẫn giữ cảm giác **nhẹ, tự nhiên, không giống phỏng vấn**.\n\n## 1. Morning Routine\n\n**A:** Morning! How’s your day going so far?  \n**B:** Pretty good. I’m still waking up, though.  \n**A:** Same here. **Do you usually drink coffee in the morning?**  \n**B:** Yeah, almost every day. I normally have one before work.  \n**A:** **How many cups do you usually have?**  \n**B:** Usually just one. Maybe two if it’s a really busy day.  \n**A:** Fair enough. I’m definitely a two-coffee person.\n\n**Question types:** Do you usually…? / How many…?\n\n**Useful chunks:**\n\n- still waking up = vẫn còn ngái ngủ\n    \n- almost every day = gần như mỗi ngày\n    \n- Fair enough. = Cũng hợp lý.\n    \n\n## 2. Lunch Break\n\n**A:** Had lunch yet?  \n**B:** Not yet. I’m waiting for a couple of people from my team.  \n**A:** **Where do you normally have lunch?**  \n**B:** Usually somewhere near the office.  \n**A:** **Which do you prefer, eating out or bringing lunch from home?**  \n**B:** Probably eating out. It gives me a chance to get away from my desk for a while.  \n**A:** Yeah, I know what you mean.\n\n**Question types:** Where…? / Which do you prefer…?\n\n**Useful chunks:**\n\n- somewhere near the office = đâu đó gần văn phòng\n    \n- get away from my desk = rời bàn làm việc một lúc\n    \n- I know what you mean. = Tôi hiểu ý bạn.\n    \n\n## 3. Weekend\n\n**A:** How was your weekend?  \n**B:** Pretty relaxing, actually. I mostly stayed home.  \n**A:** **Did you do anything interesting?**  \n**B:** I watched a couple of movies and went out for coffee. Nothing crazy.  \n**A:** Nice. **What kind of movies do you usually watch?**  \n**B:** Mostly thrillers and crime movies.  \n**A:** Same here. **Have you ever watched a movie that really surprised you?**  \n**B:** Yeah, definitely. A few, actually.\n\n**Question types:** Did you…? / What kind of…? / Have you ever…?\n\n**Useful chunks:**\n\n- pretty relaxing = khá thư giãn\n    \n- Nothing crazy. = Không có gì đặc biệt/lớn lao.\n    \n- a couple of = một vài / hai ba\n    \n\n## 4. Commute to Work\n\n**A:** How was the traffic this morning?  \n**B:** Surprisingly okay. I got here pretty quickly.  \n**A:** Lucky you. **How long does your commute usually take?**  \n**B:** Around thirty minutes.  \n**A:** **Has your commute changed in the last few years?**  \n**B:** Yeah. It used to take about twenty minutes, but traffic has gotten much worse.  \n**A:** Tell me about it.\n\n**Question types:** How long…? / Has…changed?\n\n**Useful chunks:**\n\n- surprisingly okay = bất ngờ là khá ổn\n    \n- It used to… = Trước đây nó từng…\n    \n- Tell me about it. = Chuẩn luôn / Tôi hiểu quá mà.\n    \n\n## 5. Music After Work\n\n**A:** What do you normally do after work?  \n**B:** Usually I just go home, have dinner, and listen to music.  \n**A:** **What kind of music are you into?**  \n**B:** Mostly house and electronic music.  \n**A:** Nice. **When do you usually listen to music?**  \n**B:** Mostly when I’m driving or trying to unwind.  \n**A:** **Do you think music helps you relax?**  \n**B:** Definitely. It helps me switch off after a long day.\n\n**Question types:** What do you normally…? / What kind of…? / When…? / Do you think…?\n\n**Useful chunks:**\n\n- be into something = thích cái gì\n    \n- unwind = thư giãn sau căng thẳng\n    \n- switch off = tạm ngắt đầu óc khỏi công việc\n    \n\n## 6. PPJ — Busy Morning\n\n**A:** Busy morning?  \n**B:** Yeah, pretty busy actually. I’ve got a few things pending.  \n**A:** **What are you working on today?**  \n**B:** I’m checking some order information and following up on a few documents.  \n**A:** **What’s your main priority today?**  \n**B:** Probably finishing the report before lunch.  \n**A:** **Do you usually have this much work in the morning?**  \n**B:** Not really. Mondays are normally worse.\n\n**Question types:** What are you…? / What’s your…? / Do you usually…?\n\n**Useful chunks:**\n\n- things pending = những việc còn chờ xử lý\n    \n- main priority = ưu tiên chính\n    \n- follow up on = theo dõi / hỏi lại về\n    \n\n## 7. PPJ — Working Across Departments\n\n**A:** Which team are you working with today?  \n**B:** Mostly Warehouse and Purchasing.  \n**A:** **What are you working on together?**  \n**B:** We’re checking a quantity mismatch.  \n**A:** Ah, got it. **Does that happen often?**  \n**B:** Not that often, luckily.  \n**A:** **How do you normally handle it when it happens?**  \n**B:** We check the documents first and then confirm the numbers with the relevant teams.\n\n**Question types:** Which…? / What…? / How often…? / How…?\n\n**Useful chunks:**\n\n- quantity mismatch = chênh lệch số lượng\n    \n- not that often = không thường xuyên lắm\n    \n- relevant teams = các bộ phận liên quan\n    \n\n## 8. PPJ — Supplier Issue\n\n**A:** You look busy. Everything okay?  \n**B:** Yeah, just dealing with a supplier issue.  \n**A:** Oh. **What happened?**  \n**B:** They pushed the delivery back by two days.  \n**A:** That’s annoying. **Has this happened before?**  \n**B:** A couple of times, unfortunately.  \n**A:** **What are you going to do now?**  \n**B:** I’m going to check with Production first and then follow up with the supplier.\n\n**Question types:** What happened? / Has this happened before? / What are you going to…?\n\n**Useful chunks:**\n\n- deal with an issue = xử lý vấn đề\n    \n- push the delivery back = lùi lịch giao hàng\n    \n- unfortunately = tiếc là / không may là\n    \n\n## 9. PPJ — Technology and AI\n\n**A:** Have you tried the new tool yet?  \n**B:** Yeah, a few times.  \n**A:** **How are you finding it?**  \n**B:** Pretty useful so far. It saves me a lot of repetitive work.  \n**A:** **What do you mainly use it for?**  \n**B:** Mostly checking information and drafting summaries.  \n**A:** **Do you think AI will change the way we work?**  \n**B:** Definitely. I think it’ll handle more repetitive tasks, but people will still need to make the final decisions.\n\n**Question types:** Have you…? / How…? / What…? / Do you think…?\n\n**Useful chunks:**\n\n- How are you finding it? = Bạn thấy nó thế nào?\n    \n- repetitive work = công việc lặp đi lặp lại\n    \n- final decision = quyết định cuối cùng\n    \n\n## 10. PPJ — Learning and Future Plans\n\n**A:** Is there anything new you’d like to learn this year?  \n**B:** Yeah, I’d like to improve my English and learn more about automation.  \n**A:** Nice. **Why do you want to improve your English?**  \n**B:** Mainly because I want to communicate more confidently at work.  \n**A:** **How important is English for your job now?**  \n**B:** Pretty important, especially when working with overseas suppliers.  \n**A:** **Would you like to use English more often at work in the future?**  \n**B:** Definitely. I think the more I use it, the more natural it’ll become.\n\n**Question types:** Would you like…? / Why…? / How important…?\n\n**Useful chunks:**\n\n- communicate confidently = giao tiếp tự tin\n    \n- overseas supplier = nhà cung cấp nước ngoài\n    \n- the more…, the more… = càng… thì càng…\n    \n\n## Speaking Pattern cần học viên bắt chước\n\nĐừng chỉ luyện từng câu hỏi. Cho học viên giữ flow:\n\n> **A:** How was your weekend?  \n> **B:** Pretty good actually. I went out with some friends.  \n> **A:** **Oh, nice. Where did you go?**  \n> **B:** We went to a new café.  \n> **A:** **Any good?**  \n> **B:** Yeah, surprisingly good. **How about you?**\n\nCông thức:\n\n**Open → Answer → React → Follow-up → Detail → Return question**\n\nĐây là rhythm nên trở thành phản xạ chính cho daily chit-chat và PPJ small talk.","x":20040,"y":19520,"width":720,"height":5025},
		{"id":"c6aef95b52932f40","type":"text","text":"# VOCAB SHEET — 160 DAILY SPEAKING COLLOCATIONS\n\n**6 chủ đề × 10 collocations**, tập trung vào những cụm có thể dùng trực tiếp trong small talk, kể chuyện, chia sẻ trải nghiệm và hội thoại hằng ngày. Các nhóm chủ đề cũng bám khá sát các mảng giao tiếp trong _Compelling Conversations_: daily habits, family, friends, food, travel, entertainment, work, stress, opinions và change.\n\n## 1. Daily Routine — Sinh hoạt hằng ngày\n\n|#|Collocation|Nghĩa tiếng Việt|Ngữ cảnh sử dụng|Example|\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n|1|**wake up early**|thức dậy sớm|Nói về lịch sinh hoạt|I usually **wake up early** on weekdays.|\n|2|**get out of bed**|ra khỏi giường|Nói về buổi sáng|I didn't want to **get out of bed** this morning.|\n|3|**get ready for work**|chuẩn bị đi làm|Morning routine|It takes me 30 minutes to **get ready for work**.|\n|4|**have breakfast**|ăn sáng|Thói quen ăn uống|I normally **have breakfast** at home.|\n|5|**leave the house**|ra khỏi nhà|Lịch trình hằng ngày|I usually **leave the house** around seven.|\n|6|**take a shower**|tắm|Sinh hoạt cá nhân|I always **take a shower** before work.|\n|7|**take a short break**|nghỉ một chút|Khi học/làm việc|I need to **take a short break**.|\n|8|**get some rest**|nghỉ ngơi|Khi mệt|I'm going home to **get some rest**.|\n|9|**go to bed late**|đi ngủ muộn|Nói về giấc ngủ|I **went to bed late** last night.|\n|10|**stick to a routine**|duy trì lịch sinh hoạt|Thói quen ổn định|I find it hard to **stick to a routine**.|\n\n## 2. Time & Plans — Thời gian và kế hoạch\n\n|#|Collocation|Nghĩa tiếng Việt|Ngữ cảnh sử dụng|Example|\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n|11|**make plans**|lên kế hoạch|Weekend / social plans|Have you **made any plans** for Saturday?|\n|12|**change my plans**|thay đổi kế hoạch|Có việc phát sinh|I had to **change my plans** at the last minute.|\n|13|**have some free time**|có thời gian rảnh|Small talk|I'll **have some free time** tonight.|\n|14|**spend time doing something**|dành thời gian làm gì|Nói về sở thích|I **spend a lot of time watching movies**.|\n|15|**save time**|tiết kiệm thời gian|Routine / productivity|Taking the train can **save time**.|\n|16|**waste time**|lãng phí thời gian|Nhận xét hoạt động|I don't want to **waste time** waiting.|\n|17|**run out of time**|hết thời gian|Khi không kịp|We **ran out of time** before finishing.|\n|18|**be in a hurry**|đang vội|Hằng ngày|Sorry, I'm **in a bit of a hurry**.|\n|19|**arrive on time**|đến đúng giờ|Cuộc hẹn|I always try to **arrive on time**.|\n|20|**take your time**|cứ từ từ|Trấn an người khác|**Take your time**. There's no rush.|\n\n## 3. Feelings & Mood — Cảm xúc và tâm trạng\n\n|#|Collocation|Nghĩa tiếng Việt|Ngữ cảnh sử dụng|Example|\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n|21|**feel tired**|cảm thấy mệt|Daily small talk|I **feel really tired** today.|\n|22|**feel stressed out**|cảm thấy rất căng thẳng|Công việc/cuộc sống|I've been **feeling stressed out** lately.|\n|23|**feel much better**|cảm thấy tốt hơn nhiều|Sau khi nghỉ/bệnh|I'm **feeling much better** now.|\n|24|**be in a good mood**|tâm trạng tốt|Small talk|You're **in a good mood** today.|\n|25|**be in a bad mood**|tâm trạng không tốt|Mô tả cảm xúc|He was **in a bad mood** this morning.|\n|26|**get upset**|buồn/bực|Khi có chuyện không vui|Don't **get upset** over something so small.|\n|27|**get excited about**|háo hức về|Plans/events|I'm **getting excited about** the trip.|\n|28|**feel nervous about**|lo lắng về|Trước sự kiện|I **feel nervous about** tomorrow's presentation.|\n|29|**calm down**|bình tĩnh lại|Tình huống căng thẳng|I went outside to **calm down**.|\n|30|**cheer someone up**|làm ai vui lên|Bạn bè buồn|Let's do something to **cheer her up**.|\n\n## 4. Friends & Social Life — Bạn bè và giao tiếp xã hội\n\n|#|Collocation|Nghĩa tiếng Việt|Ngữ cảnh sử dụng|Example|\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n|31|**make friends**|kết bạn|Nơi mới/lớp mới|It's easy to **make friends** there.|\n|32|**meet new people**|gặp người mới|Social life|I enjoy **meeting new people**.|\n|33|**hang out with friends**|đi chơi với bạn|Casual conversation|I usually **hang out with friends** on Friday nights.|\n|34|**keep in touch**|giữ liên lạc|Bạn cũ|We still **keep in touch** after university.|\n|35|**lose touch**|mất liên lạc|Quan hệ lâu ngày|We **lost touch** a few years ago.|\n|36|**get along well**|hòa hợp|Bạn bè/đồng nghiệp|We **get along really well**.|\n|37|**have a lot in common**|có nhiều điểm chung|Quan hệ|We **have a lot in common**.|\n|38|**have a good time**|có khoảng thời gian vui vẻ|Đi chơi/event|We **had a great time** last night.|\n|39|**catch up with someone**|gặp để cập nhật chuyện cũ|Bạn lâu không gặp|Let's **catch up over coffee** sometime.|\n|40|**invite someone over**|mời ai đến nhà|Bạn bè/gia đình|We're **inviting some friends over** tonight.|\n\n## 5. Family & Relationships — Gia đình và các mối quan hệ\n\n|#|Collocation|Nghĩa tiếng Việt|Ngữ cảnh sử dụng|Example|\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n|41|**spend time with family**|dành thời gian với gia đình|Weekend/holiday|I try to **spend more time with my family**.|\n|42|**be close to someone**|thân thiết với ai|Quan hệ|I'm very **close to my sister**.|\n|43|**get married**|kết hôn|Life events|They **got married** last year.|\n|44|**start a family**|lập gia đình/có con|Future plans|They want to **start a family** soon.|\n|45|**raise children**|nuôi dạy con|Parenting|It's not easy to **raise children**.|\n|46|**have an argument**|có cuộc cãi nhau|Quan hệ|We **had an argument** yesterday.|\n|47|**sort things out**|giải quyết ổn thỏa vấn đề|Sau mâu thuẫn|We talked and **sorted things out**.|\n|48|**support each other**|hỗ trợ lẫn nhau|Relationship|Good families **support each other**.|\n|49|**trust someone completely**|hoàn toàn tin tưởng ai|Quan hệ sâu|I **trust her completely**.|\n|50|**stay in touch with relatives**|giữ liên lạc với họ hàng|Gia đình xa|I **stay in touch with my relatives** online.|\n\n## 6. Food & Eating — Ăn uống\n\n|#|Collocation|Nghĩa tiếng Việt|Ngữ cảnh sử dụng|Example|\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n|51|**grab a bite**|ăn nhanh gì đó|Casual|Let's **grab a bite** before the movie.|\n|52|**have a quick lunch**|ăn trưa nhanh|Ngày bận|I only had time to **have a quick lunch**.|\n|53|**cook dinner**|nấu bữa tối|Home routine|I usually **cook dinner** for my family.|\n|54|**eat out**|ăn ngoài|Restaurant plans|We **eat out** once or twice a week.|\n|55|**order food**|gọi món/đặt đồ ăn|Restaurant/delivery|Let's **order some food** tonight.|\n|56|**try something new**|thử món/thứ mới|Food/travel|I like to **try something new** when I travel.|\n|57|**have a sweet tooth**|thích đồ ngọt|Food preference|I **have a really sweet tooth**.|\n|58|**skip a meal**|bỏ bữa|Busy routine|I sometimes **skip breakfast**.|\n|59|**feel hungry**|cảm thấy đói|Everyday speech|I'm **getting hungry**.|\n|60|**be full**|no|Sau khi ăn|I'm **completely full**. I can't eat any more.|\n\n## 7. Shopping & Money — Mua sắm và tiền bạc\n\n|#|Collocation|Nghĩa tiếng Việt|Ngữ cảnh sử dụng|Example|\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n|61|**go shopping**|đi mua sắm|Free time|I'm **going shopping** this weekend.|\n|62|**do the grocery shopping**|đi mua thực phẩm|Household routine|I usually **do the grocery shopping** on Sunday.|\n|63|**buy something online**|mua hàng online|Shopping|I usually **buy clothes online**.|\n|64|**spend money on**|tiêu tiền vào|Financial habits|I **spend too much money on** coffee.|\n|65|**save money**|tiết kiệm tiền|Plans|I'm trying to **save money** for a trip.|\n|66|**waste money on**|phí tiền vào|Opinion|Don't **waste money on** things you don't need.|\n|67|**compare prices**|so sánh giá|Shopping decision|I always **compare prices** before buying electronics.|\n|68|**get a good deal**|mua được giá hời|Shopping|I **got a really good deal** on this laptop.|\n|69|**pay in cash**|trả bằng tiền mặt|Payment|Do you want to **pay in cash** or by card?|\n|70|**be worth the money**|đáng tiền|Review/recommendation|It's expensive, but it's **worth the money**.|\n\n## 8. Travel & Transport — Du lịch và di chuyển\n\n|#|Collocation|Nghĩa tiếng Việt|Ngữ cảnh sử dụng|Example|\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n|71|**take a trip**|đi một chuyến|Travel plans|We're **taking a short trip** next month.|\n|72|**go on vacation**|đi nghỉ|Holiday|I'm **going on vacation** next week.|\n|73|**book a hotel**|đặt khách sạn|Trip planning|We need to **book a hotel** soon.|\n|74|**book a flight**|đặt vé máy bay|Travel|Have you **booked your flight** yet?|\n|75|**pack your bags**|đóng hành lý|Before travel|I haven't **packed my bags** yet.|\n|76|**catch a bus**|bắt xe buýt|Transport|I usually **catch the bus** at 7:30.|\n|77|**miss the train**|lỡ tàu|Travel problem|I **missed the train** this morning.|\n|78|**get stuck in traffic**|bị kẹt xe|Commute|We **got stuck in traffic** for an hour.|\n|79|**ask for directions**|hỏi đường|Traveling|We had to **ask for directions**.|\n|80|**explore a new place**|khám phá nơi mới|Travel|I love **exploring new places**.|\n\n## 9. Health & Fitness — Sức khỏe và vận động\n\n|#|Collocation|Nghĩa tiếng Việt|Ngữ cảnh sử dụng|Example|\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n|81|**stay healthy**|giữ sức khỏe|Lifestyle|I exercise regularly to **stay healthy**.|\n|82|**get enough sleep**|ngủ đủ|Health habits|I don't **get enough sleep** during the week.|\n|83|**eat healthy food**|ăn đồ lành mạnh|Diet|I'm trying to **eat healthier food**.|\n|84|**do some exercise**|tập thể dục|Daily health|I need to **do more exercise**.|\n|85|**go for a walk**|đi bộ|Relaxation|Let's **go for a walk** after dinner.|\n|86|**work out regularly**|tập luyện đều đặn|Gym|I **work out regularly** now.|\n|87|**lose weight**|giảm cân|Fitness|I'm trying to **lose a little weight**.|\n|88|**gain weight**|tăng cân|Health|I always **gain weight** during the holidays.|\n|89|**feel under the weather**|cảm thấy hơi không khỏe|Casual illness|I'm **feeling a bit under the weather** today.|\n|90|**take care of yourself**|chăm sóc bản thân|Advice|You need to **take better care of yourself**.|\n\n## 10. Work & Study — Công việc và học tập\n\n|#|Collocation|Nghĩa tiếng Việt|Ngữ cảnh sử dụng|Example|\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n|91|**have a busy day**|có ngày bận rộn|Small talk at work|I've **had a really busy day**.|\n|92|**get some work done**|làm được một số việc|Productivity|I need to **get some work done** tonight.|\n|93|**meet a deadline**|kịp deadline|Work/study|We need to **meet the deadline**.|\n|94|**miss a deadline**|trễ deadline|Work/study|I don't want to **miss another deadline**.|\n|95|**take notes**|ghi chú|Meeting/class|I always **take notes** during meetings.|\n|96|**learn something new**|học điều mới|Development|I **learn something new** every day.|\n|97|**make progress**|có tiến bộ|Learning/project|I'm finally **making progress** with my English.|\n|98|**pay attention**|chú ý|Classroom/conversation|You need to **pay attention** to the details.|\n|99|**ask for help**|nhờ giúp đỡ|Work/study|Don't be afraid to **ask for help**.|\n|100|**take responsibility for**|chịu trách nhiệm về|Work/life|You need to **take responsibility for your decisions**.|\n\n## 11. Conversation & Communication — Giao tiếp\n\n|#|Collocation|Nghĩa tiếng Việt|Ngữ cảnh sử dụng|Example|\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n|101|**start a conversation**|bắt đầu cuộc trò chuyện|Social situations|It's sometimes hard to **start a conversation**.|\n|102|**keep the conversation going**|duy trì cuộc trò chuyện|Speaking skill|Ask follow-up questions to **keep the conversation going**.|\n|103|**ask a question**|đặt câu hỏi|Mọi tình huống|Can I **ask you a question**?|\n|104|**answer honestly**|trả lời thành thật|Discussion|Just **answer honestly**.|\n|105|**tell a story**|kể chuyện|Casual speaking|He loves **telling funny stories**.|\n|106|**give an example**|đưa ví dụ|Giải thích ý|Can you **give me an example**?|\n|107|**make a point**|đưa ra một luận điểm|Discussion|She **made a good point**.|\n|108|**express your opinion**|bày tỏ ý kiến|Discussion|Don't be afraid to **express your opinion**.|\n|109|**change the subject**|đổi chủ đề|Conversation management|Let's **change the subject**.|\n|110|**have a chat**|trò chuyện|Casual|We **had a quick chat** after work.|\n\n## 12. Opinions & Reactions — Ý kiến và phản ứng\n\n|#|Collocation|Nghĩa tiếng Việt|Ngữ cảnh sử dụng|Example|\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n|111|**agree with someone**|đồng ý với ai|Discussion|I completely **agree with you**.|\n|112|**disagree with someone**|không đồng ý với ai|Discussion|I **disagree with him** on that point.|\n|113|**have a different opinion**|có quan điểm khác|Polite disagreement|I **have a slightly different opinion**.|\n|114|**make sense**|hợp lý|Phản ứng|Yeah, that **makes sense**.|\n|115|**sound like a good idea**|nghe có vẻ là ý hay|Proposal|That **sounds like a good idea**.|\n|116|**have a point**|nói có lý|Acknowledging|You **have a point** there.|\n|117|**see your point**|hiểu quan điểm của bạn|Discussion|I **see your point**, but I'm not fully convinced.|\n|118|**change your mind**|thay đổi ý định/quan điểm|Decisions|What made you **change your mind**?|\n|119|**make up your mind**|quyết định|Choice|I can't **make up my mind**.|\n|120|**strongly believe**|tin tưởng mạnh mẽ|Strong opinion|I **strongly believe** education matters.|\n\n## 13. Problems & Decisions — Vấn đề và quyết định\n\n|#|Collocation|Nghĩa tiếng Việt|Ngữ cảnh sử dụng|Example|\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n|121|**have a problem**|gặp vấn đề|General|We **have a small problem**.|\n|122|**deal with a problem**|xử lý vấn đề|Work/life|I'll **deal with the problem** tomorrow.|\n|123|**solve a problem**|giải quyết vấn đề|Problem-solving|We need to **solve this problem** quickly.|\n|124|**make a decision**|đưa ra quyết định|Choices|I need more time to **make a decision**.|\n|125|**make a mistake**|mắc lỗi|Daily/work|Everyone **makes mistakes**.|\n|126|**learn from your mistakes**|học từ sai lầm|Reflection|The important thing is to **learn from your mistakes**.|\n|127|**take a risk**|chấp nhận rủi ro|Decisions|Sometimes you have to **take a risk**.|\n|128|**avoid a problem**|tránh vấn đề|Prevention|Planning ahead can **avoid a lot of problems**.|\n|129|**find a solution**|tìm giải pháp|Problem-solving|Let's **find a solution** together.|\n|130|**think things through**|suy nghĩ kỹ mọi chuyện|Before decisions|I need to **think things through** first.|\n\n## 14. Technology & Social Media — Công nghệ và mạng xã hội\n\n|#|Collocation|Nghĩa tiếng Việt|Ngữ cảnh sử dụng|Example|\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n|131|**check your phone**|kiểm tra điện thoại|Daily habit|I **check my phone** too often.|\n|132|**send a message**|gửi tin nhắn|Communication|I'll **send you a message** later.|\n|133|**make a video call**|gọi video|Friends/work|We **made a video call** last night.|\n|134|**scroll through social media**|lướt mạng xã hội|Free time|I spend too much time **scrolling through social media**.|\n|135|**post a photo**|đăng ảnh|Social media|She **posted a photo** from her trip.|\n|136|**share a link**|chia sẻ đường link|Online communication|Can you **share the link** with me?|\n|137|**download an app**|tải ứng dụng|Technology|You need to **download the app** first.|\n|138|**charge your phone**|sạc điện thoại|Everyday tech|I forgot to **charge my phone**.|\n|139|**connect to Wi-Fi**|kết nối Wi-Fi|Travel/home|I can't **connect to the Wi-Fi**.|\n|140|**go offline**|ngắt mạng/tạm rời online|Digital break|Sometimes I just want to **go offline** for a day.|\n\n## 15. Movies, Music & Entertainment — Giải trí\n\n|#|Collocation|Nghĩa tiếng Việt|Ngữ cảnh sử dụng|Example|\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n|141|**watch a movie**|xem phim|Free time|Do you want to **watch a movie** tonight?|\n|142|**listen to music**|nghe nhạc|Daily life|I **listen to music** while driving.|\n|143|**watch a TV series**|xem series|Entertainment|I'm **watching a new TV series** right now.|\n|144|**go to a concert**|đi concert|Music|I'd love to **go to a concert** this year.|\n|145|**play video games**|chơi game|Leisure|I sometimes **play video games** after work.|\n|146|**read a book**|đọc sách|Leisure|I'm **reading a really good book** at the moment.|\n|147|**watch something again**|xem lại|Favorite content|I'd definitely **watch that movie again**.|\n|148|**have good taste in music**|có gu âm nhạc tốt|Music discussion|You **have really good taste in music**.|\n|149|**recommend a movie**|giới thiệu phim|Small talk|Can you **recommend a good movie**?|\n|150|**be a big fan of**|rất thích / là fan lớn của|Preferences|I'm **a big fan of** science fiction.|\n\n## 16. Free Time, Weekend & Lifestyle — Thời gian rảnh và lối sống\n\n|#|Collocation|Nghĩa tiếng Việt|Ngữ cảnh sử dụng|Example|\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n|151|**have a relaxing weekend**|có cuối tuần thư giãn|Monday small talk|I **had a really relaxing weekend**.|\n|152|**stay at home**|ở nhà|Weekend plans|I'm probably going to **stay at home** tonight.|\n|153|**go out for dinner**|ra ngoài ăn tối|Social plans|We're **going out for dinner** tonight.|\n|154|**spend time outdoors**|dành thời gian ngoài trời|Lifestyle|I try to **spend more time outdoors**.|\n|155|**pick up a hobby**|bắt đầu một sở thích|Lifestyle change|I want to **pick up a new hobby**.|\n|156|**take it easy**|thư giãn, đừng làm quá sức|Casual advice|I'm just going to **take it easy** this weekend.|\n|157|**stay up late**|thức khuya|Weekend/nightlife|I **stayed up late** watching a movie.|\n|158|**sleep in**|ngủ nướng|Weekend|I love **sleeping in** on Sundays.|\n|159|**make the most of something**|tận dụng tối đa|Time/experience|Let's **make the most of the weekend**.|\n|160|**try something different**|thử điều gì khác|Lifestyle/adventure|I want to **try something different** this year.|\n\n## Cách học để biến 160 cụm này thành speaking reflex\n\nKhông nên học:\n\n> wake = thức dậy  \n> plan = kế hoạch  \n> decision = quyết định\n\nNên học nguyên cụm:\n\n> **wake up early**  \n> **change my plans**  \n> **make a decision**\n\nVới mỗi collocation, luyện theo pattern:\n\n> **Trigger → Collocation → Personal detail → Follow-up**\n\nVí dụ:\n\n**Weekend?**\n\n> I usually **sleep in** on Sunday.  \n> I normally wake up around 9.  \n> How about you?\n\n**Work today?**\n\n> I've **had a pretty busy day**.  \n> I had three meetings this morning.  \n> What about you?\n\n**Travel?**\n\n> I love **exploring new places**.  \n> I went to Da Nang a few months ago.  \n> Where did you go on your last trip?\n\nCách này giúp collocation chuyển từ **“từ vựng mình biết” → “cụm mình bật ra được khi nói”**.","x":18760,"y":9760,"width":1088,"height":8373},
		{"id":"8166b637ae6a3f07","type":"text","text":"![[Pasted image 20260825163052.png]]","x":20098,"y":10089,"width":250,"height":487},
